// The boss journal: Vesper, the Mirror Knight, keeper of the Argent Hall, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
// Vesper never speaks, so there are no lines from her. Move keys are cast names (the journal fires on the cast bar's name).
// The idea every page carries: every hero has a reflection in the glass at the mirrored spot, and whatever strikes the reflection strikes the hero, so
// the question on every page is where the reflection is standing. A danger painted in the glass is read in the glass, and the hall's in the hall.
// The Glass Knight is her reflection (the knight in the glass, who at first does what she does). Out of Step is the opener that lets her act alone: the
// step out of line is the tell for Counterpoint, Cross-Cut and Silver Thread (and Backhand and Two Threads on Hard).
// Through the Glass is the wall (two heroes pulled through, never the shield), Shared Reflection its rounds of soaks and The Mirror Turns its release;
// Through the Glass Again is the Hard-only trade mid-wall. The Line is the last act (the mirror is a line through the hall, set by where the shield stands).
// Fracture, the slow wound a struck reflection leaves, has no cast of its own: it lives inside Lay Glass, Counterpoint and Trading Places.
// Hairline Fracture (the wall's raidwide), The Hall (the first act's opener) and the Hand Mirror's shatter are folded into their neighbours.
// Last Reflection is the enrage and has its own page, as the other bosses' enrages do.
// Double Glass, Two Threads, Hand Mirror, Backhand and Through the Glass Again are the Hard-only pages (hard: true).
// Lagging Glass, Wrong Hand and Trading Places are the Extreme-only pages (extreme: true); Trading Places is the Evenfall Dance under its Extreme name.
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).vesper = {
  // the first time this hero meets her (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "A knight in mirror-bright plate with no face, only a smooth visor that shows whoever looks, and a great blade carried point-down. Across the glass wall stood a second one of her, moving as she moved. My reflection stood in the glass too, and what struck it struck me.",
    gloomsworn: "A keeper at a gate, with a mirror where her face should be. Past the glass wall a second keeper copied her. Mine was there too, standing where I stood, only backwards, and whatever it took, I took. The dead have no reflections, I had always heard.",
    thresher: "A tall lady in silver plate, dear, with no face at all, just a visor that shows you your own. Beyond the glass stood a second lady keeping the very same steps. I found my reflection waiting there, on time, at the mirrored spot, and whatever it met, I met.",
    bloodreaver: "The silver one has no face, only a visor that shows you yours, and a sword she carries point-down like a cane! A second one of her stood in the glass copying her. So did a second me, and every wound it took came out of my hide. Marvellous, and unfair.",
    runeguard: "The Mirror Knight: faceless, her visor showing whoever looks into it, her blade carried point-down. Beyond the glass wall stands her reflection, and the first thing I set down was that I have one too, at the mirrored place. What strikes it strikes me.",
    gorgemaw: "A hostess in polished silver with no face, a visor like the back of a spoon, and a long blade held point-down. Across the glass, the same dish served again: a second knight, copying her. My own reflection was seated there too, and whatever was served to it, I tasted.",
    oathbroker: "The keeper of this gate is a knight in mirror-plate, faceless, whose visor shows only the one who reads it. A second knight stands in the glass to her terms. By a clause nobody read to me, my reflection stands at the mirrored place, and any blow upon it is charged to me.",
    ironpalm: "She has no face, only a visor that gives back whoever looks. She carried a long blade point-down and said nothing. In the glass stood a second one of her, and a second one of me. Whatever was said to it was said to me.",
    ranger: "Tall, silver, no face. Her visor shows you yourself. A second one of her stood in the glass, moving the same. My reflection stood there too, on the mirrored spot. What hit it hit me. Two places to watch, and one of them is behind a wall.",
    shadowblade: "The mark wears a mirror where her face should be, which I find tasteless. In the glass stood a second mark, copying her, and a second me, quite uninvited, at the mirrored spot. What touched my shadow touched me. I have never been so thoroughly followed.",
    pyromancer: "A knight with no face and a visor like a polished pan, and behind a wall of glass a second knight copying her! And a second ME, at the mirrored spot, standing in whatever the floor did. If it burned, I burned. I had to share the spotlight with myself.",
    thermist: "A faceless knight in silver plate, a visor that shows who looks. Another in the glass, copying her. Then the shock of it: my own reflection, at the mirrored spot, taking whatever I would take, and I ran cold, and then hot, because it can be hurt, and so can I.",
    driftblade: "A silver knight with a mirror for a visor, and a second in the glass that copied every move she made, and me in the glass too, running a mirrored path beside my own, and whatever caught it caught me, and I could not outrun it, because it ran with me.",
    alembist: "Specimen: one knight, silver plate, no face, a visor that shows the observer. Observed: a second knight in the glass, copying her. Complication: I have a reflection as well, at the mirrored spot, and whatever hits it hits me. Label for the shelf: two bodies, one patient.",
    threadweaver: "A silver lady in plate that looks stitched from mirror, faceless, with a blade carried point-down. In the glass stood a second lady, matching her seam for seam. And a second me, hem to hem with the first, because whatever struck it struck me.",
    marionettist: "A new house, and what a set: a hall of moon-silver with a mirror wall, our leading lady in plate with no face, and in the glass her understudy, copying every move. And in the glass, my own understudy, at the mirrored mark, taking every blow I would. (The Doll has a double now too.)",
    cleric: "Poor lady, with no face and a mirror for a visor, and a second knight behind the glass doing everything she did. I looked for my own reflection and found it at the mirrored place. What struck it struck me. I asked the others to look for theirs, and mind where it stood.",
    grovekeeper: "An old gate and its keeper: a knight with no face, silver plate, a visor that shows you your own. Behind the glass wall stood another like her, moving with her. My own double stood in the glass too. What fell on it fell on me, like rain on a roof.",
    chronomancer: "A knight with a mirror for a face, and in the glass a second who did what she did a breath behind, or a breath ahead; I could not decide. My own reflection stood there at the mirrored spot. I have met myself before, but never one who shared my bruises.",
    tidecaller: "A still pool of a knight, silver and faceless, with a visor that shows whoever leans over it, and in the glass another, moving as she moved. My own reflection lay in the glass too, at the mirrored place, and whatever troubled its water troubled mine.",
    lanternkeeper: "A tall hall of moon-silver, and a knight in it with no face and a visor that gives back every lamp. In the glass stood a second knight, and a second lantern for every lantern. I stood in the glass too, and what struck that one struck this one. Two of me to keep lit.",
    kenseier: "A swordswoman in plate bright as a mirror, no face, a great blade carried point-down. In the glass, a second swordswoman. My own reflection stood there at the mirrored place, and a cut that falls on it falls on me, with no difference in the beat. She gives each of us two bodies to guard.",
    houndmaster: "Silver lass in the hall, no face, a visor that shows you back to yourself. A second one in the glass, copying her. Rust and Ash sat down and barked at their own doubles, and I'd have joined them. Mine stood in the glass too, on the mirrored spot. What got it got me.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats her
  win: {
    bastion: "She lowered the blade and knelt, and her visor showed my own face as plainly as ever. The glass went dark. Every one of us stood in one place, one body each, and the gate was open. Report: we kept our footing on both sides of the wall.",
    gloomsworn: "The keeper knelt and her reflection faded into the glass, and the hall had only one of each of us in it at last. I looked for mine and it was gone. I felt a loss, which surprised me. The gate stood open, and I let the others through first.",
    thresher: "Down she went, dear, in a bow, and the second lady in the glass bowed with her and went out like a lamp. Everyone had arrived on time, in one piece apiece. Her hour was kept. The gate swung open, quite pleased with us.",
    bloodreaver: "She fell, and her double fell with her, and the hall was quiet. I counted my scars and could not tell which ones my reflection had earned for me. Fair enough, my friend! A wound is a wound, and I drank every one.",
    runeguard: "The Mirror Knight is fallen, and the margin is full: every glass danger read, the mirror moved twice, the last line drawn where our shield stood. The gate is open. At the very end her visor showed only the hall. I wrote that down last.",
    gorgemaw: "She knelt, the dish was cleared, and the second serving melted off the glass like sugar. I had tasted every blow twice, and I wanted a third helping of the keeper. A cold, bright kitchen, and the gate beyond it stood open.",
    oathbroker: "The keeper has defaulted, and the gate is released. Her counterpart in the glass was dissolved under the same instrument. Each of us has left the hall in a single body, with no reflection bearing our liabilities. I declined to countersign the mirror.",
    ironpalm: "She knelt and the glass was only glass. Four of us stood in the hall, one body each. Nobody spoke for a while. The gate opened without a sound. Her visor had answered whoever looked into it to the end, and I looked once more before I left.",
    ranger: "Down. Glass went dark, her double with it. One body each again. Gate open. I looked for my reflection on the way out and found only the hall. Fewer places to watch.",
    shadowblade: "She knelt at the end, and the glass took her double, and for one moment the room held nothing that looked like me. I rather missed it. The gate opened at the next breath, and I walked through ahead of my own shadow.",
    pyromancer: "She fell, and the second knight went out in the glass like a snuffed candle, and my reflection stopped following me about the stage! I took a bow to a blank wall. It was the best audience I have had, and it bowed back.",
    thermist: "Down, and the glass went still. One body each. I ran cold in the quiet, then warm with it. Whatever stood in my reflection's place was only floor. The gate stood open, silver, and a good deal brighter than anything behind us.",
    driftblade: "She went down and the glass went dark all at once, and I was running before it went, round the hall and round again, one body of me at last and no second one lagging behind, and the gate was open by the time I stopped.",
    alembist: "Logged: one knight, felled; one reflection, dissolved with her. All flasks whole. My reflection left no sample. Note for the shelf: do not trust a reagent that matches you. The gate is open, and I am once again exactly one alchemist.",
    threadweaver: "Both knights are down and the glass is a plain wall. I counted my stitches, one of each, no doubles on a loose thread. Everyone was whole, which is rarer than it sounds. Come along, all of you, the gate is open.",
    marionettist: "Final curtain. Our leading lady knelt, her understudy took the bow in the glass, and then the glass dimmed on both. One company, no doubles. (The Doll refused to come out until she was sure there was only one of her.)",
    cleric: "She knelt, and her reflection knelt, and then there was only the hall. I counted my friends and each of them was in one place, whole. I said a prayer for the lady, who had only ever wanted to guard a gate. It stood open when we looked.",
    grovekeeper: "The keeper is down, and the glass has gone dull, like a pond in frost. Each of my lot stood in one place, in one piece. I sat on the cold floor and let the quiet grow in. The gate stood open, and nothing beyond it looked like us.",
    chronomancer: "She went down, and the second one down with her, in the same beat, which I admired. Nothing needed undoing, and my reflection had not been in my way for a good while. I will have left the hall last, and it was a pleasure.",
    tidecaller: "The glass went still as slack water, and in it, for a moment, only the hall. She was gone. I stood at the edge of it and saw one of me, with no ripple. A calm sea gives the truest reflection.",
    lanternkeeper: "The glass went dark, and for the first time I had one lantern for one of me. She knelt. I lit a small lamp at the foot of the gate and left it there, and the knight's visor caught it and showed only light.",
    kenseier: "The swordswoman is down, and her double with her, on the same beat. I bowed to her. The glass shows the hall, one body to each of us, and no cut may fall on a second. The gate is open. She taught well, and she taught everyone twice.",
    houndmaster: "Silver lass is down and her double with her. Rust and Ash stopped barking at the wall and trotted up with just the one of each. Gate's open. Walked in and didn't look back at the glass.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Evenfall': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The sign went over one of the others and her great sweep turned to follow them. It swung in both rooms alike, so dodging the one was dodging both. The marked one stepped out once it stopped following, and the rest of us kept clear of its line.",
      gloomsworn: "An eye hung over one of us and her sweep leaned toward them, the same sweep in the glass. The marked one drew it off to empty floor, then stepped aside when it stopped following. The rest of us stood off its line. It was not meant for me, and I did not take it.",
      thresher: "One of us wears the little eye, dear, and her big sweep follows them about until the last moment. So lead it away from the others, then sidestep when it stops, and everyone else stays off the line. Both knights swing alike, so there is only one step to learn.",
      bloodreaver: "An eye over one of my friends and the sword came round after them! The glass knight swung the very same swing, so one dodge answered both. The marked one dragged it off to the edge, away from us, then stepped out sideways when it locked. I envied them the attention.",
      runeguard: "The sign is an eye over a head; the sweep follows that hero until it locks, and its mirror image falls the same in the glass. The marked hero leads it away from the party, then steps clear to the side as it locks. The rest of us stay out of its line.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess carved at one guest, and the carving followed them round the room until the last instant. The glass served the very same cut, so one step out of the hall's was a step out of both. The marked one led it off to a bare corner and slid aside.",
      oathbroker: "The instrument names one party by an eye overhead, and the sweep follows that party until it locks. The glass repeats it in identical terms, so one withdrawal satisfies both. The named party draws it clear of the rest, then steps aside as it locks.",
      ironpalm: "An eye over one of us. Her blade turned after them, wide, and the glass turned the same. The marked one walked it away from us, and when it stopped following, stepped out. One answer for both rooms. The rest of us stood off its line.",
      ranger: "Eye over one of us. Wide sweep follows them till it locks. Same sweep in the glass, no difference. Marked one walks it off to open ground, sidesteps once it settles. The rest keep clear of its arc. Narrow near her feet, wide at the far end.",
      shadowblade: "The eye picked someone else, how kind. Her great sweep followed them until it locked, and the glass swung the same, so there was nothing clever to do but step aside when it settled. I stood out of its line and let the marked one lead it far from the rest.",
      pyromancer: "The little eye went over somebody else, and the sweep followed them round the room like a spotlight! Same swing in the glass, thank goodness, so there was nothing new to read. They led it away from us and stepped out when it locked. I stood off its edge and finished my spell.",
      thermist: "Eye over someone else. Sweep tracks them. Same sweep in the glass, so one step answers both. Lead it away from the rest, sidestep when it stops tracking, and I was already at its edge, hot with relief, keeping out of its line.",
      driftblade: "The eye went over somebody else and the big sweep went after them, and the same sweep in the glass, so I ran wide of its line while they led it round and out, and the instant it stopped following they slipped off sideways, and there was nothing left for it to cut.",
      alembist: "Observed: one hero marked with an eye, a wide sweep that follows them, repeated identically in the glass. Method: the marked hero leads it off from the party and steps aside once it stops following. Everyone else off the line. One dodge covers both rooms.",
      threadweaver: "An eye over one of my stitches, and the sweep trailed after them like a loose thread. Same cut in the glass, so one sidestep saved both. They led it away from the rest of us and stepped out when it stopped following. I kept the others off the line.",
      marionettist: "A spotlight in the shape of an eye over one of the company, and our leading lady's sweep followed them about the stage. The glass repeated the same swing: no new blocking. The marked one led it into the wings, then stepped aside as it locked. (The rest of us stayed off the line.)",
      cleric: "One of my friends bore the eye, and the sweep followed them about until the last moment. The glass swings the same swing, so one step is enough for both. I asked them to lead it away from us first, then step aside, and I kept the rest of us off its line.",
      grovekeeper: "An eye over one of my lot, and a great sweep leaned after them like grass after the wind. The glass swung the same, so one step carried both. The marked one led it off to bare ground, and when it stopped following, stepped aside. The rest of us stood out of its path.",
      chronomancer: "The eye picked someone, the sweep followed them, and the glass swung the same, in step. The marked one led it away from us and stepped aside when it stopped tracking. I will have known which way they would turn; they turned that way. The rest of us stayed out of the line.",
      tidecaller: "An eye rose over one of us, and her sweep followed them the way a current follows a swimmer. The glass swung the same swing. The marked one drew it away from the rest, slowly, and stepped aside when it stopped following. The rest of us kept out of its path.",
      lanternkeeper: "One of us had the eye over their head. I picked a lantern up and set it down at the edge of the hall, away from the rest, and they led the sweep there, then stepped off when it stopped following. Same swing in the glass. One lantern was enough for both rooms.",
      kenseier: "She cuts after a long breath, and the eye shows her mark. The sweep follows until the last beat, then locks. The marked one led it away from us and stepped out on the lock. The glass swings the same, so one step answers two. The rest stood off the line.",
      houndmaster: "Eye over one of us, and her great sweep followed them like a dog after a hare. Same swing in the glass. The marked one led it off from the rest, stepped aside once it stopped following. Rust and Ash and I stayed out of its line, flanks only.",
    } },
    'Lay Glass': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Shards fell in the glass only, and the floor under my boots stayed clean. They came down on the spots where a few of the others had stood when she began, and one more at random. I watched the glass, not the hall. The ones who moved off their spot were not hurt.",
      gloomsworn: "Nothing on the hall floor, and I felt the cold of it anyway. The shards fell in the glass, on the places where a few of us had been standing when she began. A reflection can be cut, and the wound is yours. It left a slow ache behind. I have worn worse.",
      thresher: "Nothing falls on the floor, dear; it all falls in the glass, on the spots where a few of us stood when she began, and one more for luck. So don't stand where you were. Step off, and your reflection steps with you, and the little cut it would have left stays unmade.",
      bloodreaver: "The shards fell in the glass, and I felt nothing, and resented it, until one of my friends clutched their side where their reflection had been cut! The shards land where a few of us stood when she began. Leave the spot, and keep off the stray one.",
      runeguard: "The shards are drawn in the glass alone, on the places where some stood when she began, and one place besides. A reflection that stands there is struck, and the hero with it. The cure is written plainly: step off the spot, and watch the glass for the stray.",
      gorgemaw: "A dish served to the wrong table: the shards came down in the glass, on the spots where a few of my companions had been seated when she began, and one for the house. Nothing touched my own plate. Get up from your seat, and the course goes cold on an empty chair.",
      oathbroker: "The liability attaches to the reflection, not the body, and the body pays it. Shards were laid in the glass on the places where a few of us stood at the outset, and one other. The remedy is to vacate before they come down, and to read the glass, since the hall shows nothing.",
      ironpalm: "Nothing was said in the hall; it was all said in the glass, on the spots where a few of the others had stood, and one more. I read the glass for the stray and stayed off it. The others stepped off their spots, and the words fell on nobody.",
      ranger: "Shards in the glass only. They land on the spots where a few of us stood when she began, plus one stray. Nothing on the hall floor, so look up at the glass. Step off the old spot, clear of the stray too. A wound after, for whoever was late.",
      shadowblade: "The floor offered nothing, which was suspicious, and the glass offered shards at the places a few of us had been standing, and one at random. It is only polite to leave before the guest arrives. I slipped off my spot early and my reflection slipped with me.",
      pyromancer: "The hall floor stayed dark and the glass lit up with shards, right where I'd been planted when she began! Of course! I stepped off the spot, my reflection stepped off with me, and the shard came down on bare glass. The stray one I avoided with a squeak.",
      thermist: "Hall clean. Glass not. Shards on the spots where a few stood when she began, plus one stray. Step off, and your reflection steps too. Whoever dawdled took a wound that ticked on and on, and I ran cold watching, and stayed off the stray.",
      driftblade: "The floor stayed clean and the shards fell in the glass, on the spots where a few of us had been standing when she began, and I was gone from mine before the first one drew, through the gap, round the stray one, and my reflection came running after, never touched.",
      alembist: "Hypothesis: shards in the glass only, laid on the spots a few of us stood on when she began, plus one stray. Result: those who left early were untouched. Side effect: a struck reflection leaves a slow wound. Mend lightly and often.",
      threadweaver: "The shards fell where my stitches' reflections stood, in the glass only, on the spots they had when she began, plus one stray. I told them to step off and they did, tidy as pins. A struck reflection leaves a slow snag afterwards, and I mended it a little at a time.",
      marionettist: "A scene set entirely in the glass: shards fall on the marks where a few of the company stood when she began, and one more at random, while the hall stays bare. Leave your mark. (My understudy, in the glass, had already gone.) Whoever lingered took a slow bruise after.",
      cleric: "Nothing fell on the hall floor. The shards fell in the glass, on the places where a few of my friends had stood when she began, and one more. I asked them to step off early. For the one who was late, I mended a little at a time, since the wound ticks on.",
      grovekeeper: "No rain on the hall, but the glass had a hailstorm in it, over the spots where a few of my lot had stood when she began, and a stray. Move off the old spot, and you don't get wet. The slow ache for whoever lingered, I tended a little at a time.",
      chronomancer: "The shards fall in the glass, on the spots we had been standing on when she began; I will have moved off mine already. A few of us, and a stray. Whoever lingered took a slow wound afterwards, and I mended it a little at a time, so it hardly happened.",
      tidecaller: "The hall stayed dry. The glass took the storm, a handful of shards on the spots where a few of us had stood when she began, and one more at random. I led my reflection out of the way as you lead a swimmer from a rip. A struck one aches slowly.",
      lanternkeeper: "Nothing lit on the floor; the glass lit instead, over the spots where a few of us had stood when she began, and one more. I stepped off mine early. A struck reflection leaves a slow wound, so I kept a light on whoever was late.",
      kenseier: "The hall had no blow in it. The glass did: shards on the spots where a few of us had stood when she began, and one stray. The blow falls on the reflection and lands on the hero. We left our spots early. A late one earns a slow wound.",
      houndmaster: "Floor stayed clean, glass filled with shards, right where a few of us were standing when she began, and one stray. Stepped off mine early, the dogs with me, and the shards fell on bare glass. Whoever dawdled got a slow ache after. Easy once you've looked up.",
    } },
    'Twin Verdict': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She cut at me, and a beat later her reflection cut at mine. Two blows, the second lighter. I braced for the first and did not let my guard down in the gap, and I held her where the line between us ran over empty floor. Footing good both times.",
      gloomsworn: "She cut at me, and the knight in the glass cut at my own reflection a breath behind. Both landed on the same man. I drew the dark in for the first and kept it in for the second. The rest kept off the line between us. One wound would have been enough for anyone.",
      thresher: "Two bows for our shield, dear, one from her and one from her reflection a moment behind, the second a little gentler. So hold the whole step through both, and don't leave early. The rest of us stay off the line between her and our shield, or we take our share.",
      bloodreaver: "She cut me and then her reflection cut me again, a moment later, a little softer! A good pair of blows, and I took both on my own two feet. Everyone else stayed out of the line between us, so only I got the scars.",
      runeguard: "The sign is her blade lifting toward the shield, and the ward is laid in two parts: up for the first blow and not let down for the second, which falls in the glass on my reflection a moment later, and lighter. The rest of us stand off the line between.",
      gorgemaw: "Two courses for me: one from the hostess, one from her reflection a moment later, the second a little lighter. I took both without sending either back, and everyone else kept off the line between us, so no one else got fed. Rich, then thin.",
      oathbroker: "Two instalments, one in each room: the first from the keeper at me, the second from her reflection at mine, lighter, a moment later. I accepted both with a defence set to cover the pair. My co-signatories stayed off the line between us, lest they share in it.",
      ironpalm: "She spoke to me, and her reflection spoke again a moment later, more quietly. Two words. I answered the first in full and the second without dropping my arms. The others stayed off the line between her and me. Then the room was quiet.",
      ranger: "Two cuts at the shield, one from her, one from her reflection a moment behind and lighter. Both along the same line, and nobody else on it. Shield kept the guard up through the gap. Healer mended after the second, not between.",
      shadowblade: "She cut at our shield and her reflection cut at the shield's reflection a moment later, a little softer, which is what an encore is. I stayed off the line between her and them, and came in behind her when it was done. The shield took both with the guard still up.",
      pyromancer: "Two swings at the shield, one from her and one from her reflection just after, a little weaker, like an echo that had lost its nerve! I stood well off the line between them and finished my spell from the side. The shield kept their guard up between the two.",
      thermist: "Two cuts at the shield: hers, then her reflection's, a moment later and lighter. Off the line between her and them, and don't walk into the gap. The shield kept the guard up for both. I ran hot until the second landed, and cool after.",
      driftblade: "She swung at the shield and her reflection swung at the shield's reflection right behind it, lighter, and I was well off the line between her and them, round the flank and past, while the shield kept their guard up through both, which I could not have done standing still.",
      alembist: "Observed: two cuts at the shield, hers then her reflection's a moment later, the second lighter. Method: guard up for both, everyone else off the line between her and the shield, and the strong dose after the second blow, not between. It catches a careless healer.",
      threadweaver: "Two cuts for our shield, one from her and one from her reflection just behind, the second lighter. I kept the others off the line between her and them, and held my mending until after the second, so the shield was not left slack between the two.",
      marionettist: "A duet for our knight: the leading lady's cut, and her understudy's just after, in the glass, a little softer. The company stayed off the line between her and our knight, and the knight kept the guard up through both. (A short number, with one singer.)",
      cleric: "Two blows came at our knight: hers, and her reflection's just after, softer. I laid the light on them before the first, and mended after the second, not between. The rest of us kept off the line between her and our knight.",
      grovekeeper: "Two blows for my big fellow, hers and then her reflection's, close together, the second the lighter. I'd planted on them before the first, and tended after the second. The rest of my lot stayed out of the line between her and them. Hail comes in twos as well.",
      chronomancer: "She cuts at the shield, and the glass cuts just behind her, a little lighter: a double strike, with an echo that matters. The shield kept the guard up through the echo, I mended after it, and the rest of us stayed off the line. I will have heard the second before it came.",
      tidecaller: "A swell, and then a smaller swell behind it: her cut at the shield, and her reflection's cut at theirs, just after and lighter. The shield kept the guard up through both, and I waited for the second to break before I raised the water. The rest stayed off the line.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two cuts at our shield, one from her and a lighter one from her reflection just behind. I hung a light on the shield before the first and kept it burning through the second. The rest of us kept off the line between. One shield, two cuts, one lamp.",
      kenseier: "Two strokes at the shield, her own and a lighter one from her reflection just behind. I met the first on its landing with the blade and drew the ash up for the second. The others stayed off the line between us. Both were honest about their count.",
      houndmaster: "She swung at the shield-man, and her double swung again just behind, a bit lighter. Shield-man kept his guard up through both. Dogs and I stayed off the line between them, wide on the flank. Healer waited for the second before mending.",
    } },
    'Ring True': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Her blades struck the wall, and the blast came through the glass to every one of us. There is nothing to raise against it. We stood close by the healer and took it mended beforehand. It cost the whole line the same.",
      gloomsworn: "Both blades into the wall, and the blow went through the glass and found all of us at once. No post to hold against it. I stood where I was and let it pass through me, as I have let a good many things. The healer mended after.",
      thresher: "She struck the wall with both blades, dear, and the whole hall rang with it, glass and all. Nowhere to step. Everyone was already mended when it came, and tidy after. It is only a bell, and a loud one.",
      bloodreaver: "She hit the wall with both blades and every one of us felt it, glass and hall alike! Nothing to hit back at, so I took it standing, already topped up, and counted the ache. A thin scar, shared out.",
      runeguard: "The sign is the two blades lifted together; the blast follows through both rooms and reaches every one of us. No ward turns it. We were whole beforehand, and the healer set us right after. It is the plainest entry in the margin.",
      gorgemaw: "She rapped both blades on the wall like a spoon on a glass, and the whole room rang. Nobody escapes a toast. We were fed with healing beforehand, and took it standing. A thin broth, served to every chair at once.",
      oathbroker: "The levy fell on every party present, in both rooms, as she struck the wall with her blades. No clause exempts anyone, so the remedy is solvency: be mended before it lands, and again after. The healer bears the whole burden of this instrument.",
      ironpalm: "She struck the wall with both blades. It rang through the glass and through all of us. No answer to give, only a long wait for it to pass. We were already mended when it came, and the healer mended us again.",
      ranger: "Both blades on the wall. Rings through the glass, reaches everyone. No cover, no lane. Topped off before it, took it standing, mended after. Sometimes another move came hard behind it, so I kept my feet under me.",
      shadowblade: "Both blades against the wall, and the whole hall rang, the glass too. A bell has no dark corner. I stood where I was, mended in advance, and let it wash over me. A very ordinary sort of rudeness, and I forgave it.",
      pyromancer: "She struck the wall with both blades and the whole hall rang like a gong, glass and all! Nowhere to stand. I stayed where I was, got mended before it landed, and kept my chin up. The healer did the rest. A very loud little bow.",
      thermist: "Both blades on the wall. A ring through both rooms, no ground to pick. Topped off, braced, took it. Then the shaking, and mended again. Some blows are only outlasted, and I outlasted it hot-faced and quiet.",
      driftblade: "Both blades on the wall and the ring came through the glass and over all of us, and there was nowhere to run to, which is the worst thing a ring can do, so I stood, and stayed in reach of the healer, and took it already mended.",
      alembist: "Entry: both blades struck on the wall, a ring that reaches everyone in both rooms. Nothing to dodge. Everyone mended beforehand and the next dose after. Sometimes another move came hard behind it, so do not spend the whole shelf.",
      threadweaver: "Both blades on the wall, and the ring ran down every thread at once, glass and hall. Nowhere to step. I mended all of my stitches before it came, so none was slack when it landed, and mended again after. Sometimes a move followed hard behind it.",
      marionettist: "She struck the wall with both blades and the whole house rang, stalls and glass alike. Nowhere to hide, so the company stood where it stood, mended beforehand, and took the note together. (Applause for the healer, afterwards.)",
      cleric: "Both of her blades struck the wall, and the ring went through the glass and all of us. There was nothing to stand clear of. I laid the light on every one of my friends before it came, and mended what it took after.",
      grovekeeper: "A bell struck on the wall, both blades, and the sound came through the glass and found every one of us. No place to dodge a sound. I'd planted on all of my lot before it rang, and tended after. Some days only need riding out.",
      chronomancer: "Both blades on the wall, and the ring comes through the glass and over all of us together; nowhere to stand for it. We were mended before it came, I mended again after, and it will have hurt a good deal less than it might. Sometimes another move came hard behind it.",
      tidecaller: "She struck the wall with both blades, and the ring crossed the glass and the hall like a swell with no shore to break on. No hiding from it, so I raised the water before it came, and eased it after. It broke on every one of us the same.",
      lanternkeeper: "Both blades on the wall, and the ring went through the glass and reached all of us. I lit each of us before it came, one lamp apiece, and topped the wicks up after. Sometimes another move followed close behind, so I did not put the oil away.",
      kenseier: "Both blades struck the wall, and the ring came through the glass with no beat to meet and no edge to turn. A blade cannot cut a ring. We were mended in advance, and the healer set us right after. Sometimes a move came close behind it.",
      houndmaster: "She banged both blades on the wall, big noise through the glass and the lot of us. No dodging a noise. Got mended first, took it standing, mended again. The pair pinned their ears back. Sometimes another thing came hard behind it, so we didn't sit down.",
    } },
    'Out of Step': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A light blast came through the glass, and after it her reflection stood half a pace off her mark. Report: when it steps out of line, the next move is the reflection's own, and the glass shows something the hall does not. I watched it step, then read both floors.",
      gloomsworn: "A light blow went round the hall, and her reflection stepped out of line with her, a half-pace off. A watchman learns to read the one who leaves their post. When it stepped, the next move was its own. I looked to the glass, and not to her.",
      thresher: "A little knock to start, dear, and then her reflection stepped aside from her mark, just a half-step. That is the hour striking: when it steps, the next thing belongs to the glass alone, and her sword does something different. Look at both floors.",
      bloodreaver: "A light blow, nothing worth a scar, and then her reflection stepped out of line! A half pace, no more. When it steps, the next move belongs to the glass alone, and the hall and the glass hurt differently. My friend gave fair warning for once.",
      runeguard: "A light blast opens this act, and the sign that follows it is new: her reflection steps half a pace off its mark. When it does, the next move is the reflection's own, and what the glass shows is not what the hall shows. Read both floors, glass first.",
      gorgemaw: "A light appetiser, then my hostess's reflection stepped off its plate, half a pace out of line. Whenever it stepped away, the next course was the glass's own, cooked differently from hers. So I read the glass after the hall, and ate from the gap between.",
      oathbroker: "A light levy opened the act, and her reflection stepped half a pace off its mark. That is notice: the next instrument is the reflection's own, and its terms differ from the hall's. A prudent party reads both documents, and acts on the clause they share.",
      ironpalm: "A small word opened the act, and her reflection stepped out of line, half a pace. When it stepped, the next thing spoken was its own. The glass said one thing and the hall another. I kept an ear on both.",
      ranger: "Light hit to open the act. Then her reflection stepped off its mark, half a pace. That means the next move is the glass's own, different from hers. Watch for the step, then read both floors before moving.",
      shadowblade: "A small bow of a blast, and her reflection stepped out of line, which is when the manners change. After the step, the next move is the glass's own, and the hall's is different. I watched the step and looked at both floors before I moved.",
      pyromancer: "A light little blast, and then her reflection stepped half a pace out of line! The cue, as I came to see it: when it steps, the next move is the glass's own, and the hall disagrees with it. I stopped admiring the knight and read both floors.",
      thermist: "A light hit. Her reflection stepped out of line, half a pace, and I went still. That step is a warning: the next move is the glass's own, and the hall's differs. Watch for it, and then I read both floors, quickly and all at once.",
      driftblade: "A light blast to start, and then the reflection stepped half a pace out of line, and I knew the next move was hers alone, the glass different from the hall, so I stopped looking at the knight and ran my eyes over both floors, and my route after.",
      alembist: "Observed: a light blast, then the reflection steps half a pace off its mark. Inference: the next move is the glass's own, and the hall's differs. Method: read the step as the label, then check both floors before moving.",
      threadweaver: "A light blast, and her reflection stepped out of her line, a half-stitch off. After that, any move it makes is its own cloth, and the hall's differs. I watched for the step, then looked at both floors before sending my stitches anywhere.",
      marionettist: "A light blast opened the act, and the understudy stepped out of the blocking, a half-pace off its mark. The cue, as I learned: when it steps, the next scene is the glass's own, and the hall plays something else. The company read both stages.",
      cleric: "A light blast, and then the reflection stepped out of line, only half a pace. I watched for that step all night. After it, the next move belongs to the glass alone, and the hall plays differently. I asked my friends to read both floors.",
      grovekeeper: "A light blast, like a first drop of rain, and her reflection stepped out of line, half a pace. After that, the glass has its own weather. When it steps, the next move is its own, and the hall's looks different. My lot read both floors.",
      chronomancer: "A light blast, and her reflection stepped half a pace off her mark just before the next cast, which I will have noticed sooner. When it steps, the move is the glass's own, and the hall's differs. We read both floors before moving.",
      tidecaller: "A light swell to open the act, and her reflection drifted half a pace off its mark, like a boat slipping its line. When it drifts, the next move is the glass's own, and the hall's differs. I read both floors before I moved.",
      lanternkeeper: "A light blast opened the act, and the reflection stepped half a pace off its mark, the way a lamp is moved aside before the next is lit. After the step, the move is the glass's own, and the hall's differs. I looked at both floors before I moved.",
      kenseier: "A light blow, then the reflection stepped half a pace out of line. That is a rest before a stroke: the next move belongs to the glass alone, and the hall's differs. I counted the step as the first beat, and read both floors.",
      houndmaster: "Light blast to start. Then her double stepped out of line, a half pace. After that, next thing's the glass's own, and the hall's different. Watched the step and the dogs watched me. Looked at both floors before we moved.",
    } },
    'Counterpoint': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Her reflection stepped aside and then both knights cut at once: a ring close round her in the hall, a wide ring round the reflection in the glass. The safe ground was the band between. I held her where the band was whole, and the line stood in it.",
      gloomsworn: "A ring at her feet, a ring round her reflection that began further out. The ground between was the only ground, so I kept her in the middle of the hall to give us all room to stand on it. It is a narrow band for a wide crowd, and I have lived in narrower.",
      thresher: "The ring at her toes is the hall's, dear, and the quiet middle of the great ring is the glass's, so there is a band to stand on, between. Keep off her feet, and close enough that your reflection stays in the middle of the great ring. A slow ache after, for a miss.",
      bloodreaver: "A ring right round her feet for the brave and a bigger ring round her reflection in the glass for the cowards! The only safe ground was between them. I dragged her away from the walls so there was room, and stood in the band with the others, itching.",
      runeguard: "The sign is her reflection stepping aside, and the move is two rings: a small one at her feet in the hall, and in the glass a large one with a quiet middle. The safe ground is the band between, so I held her clear of the walls and the band was whole.",
      gorgemaw: "A small plate at her feet, and a great charger in the glass with a hollow in the middle. The only bare ground was the band between them. I walked her out from the wall so the band was whole, and the guests stood in it.",
      oathbroker: "Two liens: a small one on the ground at her feet in the hall, and a great one in the glass, with the middle exempt. The ground clear of both is the band between. I held her where that band lay whole on the floor, and my co-signatories stood within it.",
      ironpalm: "She said a small word at her feet and the reflection said a large one far out. Between the two, a band of quiet. We stood in the quiet. I walked her away from the walls so the band had room, and the sentence passed over us.",
      ranger: "Ring at her feet in the hall, wide ring in the glass with a quiet middle. Safe ground is the band between. Keep out of the close ring, keep your reflection inside the far one's quiet middle. Shield held her off the walls. Melee stepped back.",
      shadowblade: "A ring at the knight's feet and a great ring round the reflection, which is greedy of her. The only quiet place was the band between. I slipped back from her heels into it and waited. Where I had been, the floor lit up for nobody.",
      pyromancer: "A ring at her toes, and in the glass a huge hollow ring that only left a quiet middle! Both lit up red. I stepped back from her feet into the band between, which was rather narrow, and finished my spell standing very straight.",
      thermist: "Ring at her feet in the hall. Great ring in the glass with a hollow middle. Safe ground: the band between. I ran from her heels, then steadied once I was past the small ring. Stay close enough that your reflection stays in the hollow, and I did, breathing hard.",
      driftblade: "A ring at her feet and a great ring in the glass, hollow in the middle, so I slipped out from her heels and into the band between, close enough that my reflection stayed in the hollow, far enough that I was clear of the small ring, and stayed there until it was done.",
      alembist: "Observed: a ring at her feet in the hall, and a wide ring round her reflection in the glass with a hollow middle. Method: stand in the band between, off her feet and inside the hollow. The band is narrow, so the shield holds her off the walls. Glass hits leave a slow wound.",
      threadweaver: "A small ring at her feet and a great one round her reflection, with a hollow in it. I drew my stitches out of her heels and into the band between, close enough to stay inside the hollow. The shield walked her clear of the walls so the band was whole.",
      marionettist: "A small ring at the star's feet in the hall, and a wide hollow ring across the glass round her understudy. The company's mark was the band between. Step out of her heels, not too far. The knight walked her to centre stage so the whole band could be used.",
      cleric: "A ring right at her feet, and in the glass a larger ring with a quiet middle. The safe ground was the band between. I called everyone out from her heels and into it, and our knight kept her from the walls. A glass hit leaves a slow wound, and I mended it.",
      grovekeeper: "A ring at her feet and a wider one in the glass with a bare middle, like a ring of mushrooms in a field. The ground between was the only dry ground. My lot stepped back from her heels into it, and my big fellow kept her from the walls.",
      chronomancer: "A ring at her feet in the hall, and a great one in the glass with a hollow in the middle. The safe ground is the band between, off her heels and inside the hollow. I will have stepped back already, and I did. The shield held her away from the walls.",
      tidecaller: "A small pool at her feet and in the glass a great ring of water with a still middle. The safe place was the shallows between. We eased back from her heels into it, and the shield walked her away from the walls so there was room for us all.",
      lanternkeeper: "A ring lit at her feet in the hall, and in the glass a great ring with a dark middle. I set my lamp in the band between, off her heels and inside the dark, and the others gathered at it. The shield held her clear of the walls, so the band stayed whole.",
      kenseier: "A ring at her feet in the hall, and a wide ring in the glass with a quiet middle. The count is the step: off her heels, not too far. We stood in the band between. I held her clear of the walls, so the band stayed whole.",
      houndmaster: "Ring at her feet, big ring in the glass with a quiet middle. Safe ground's the band between. Stepped back from her heels, no further than the quiet middle, dogs with me. Shield-man had her off the walls, so there was room for the lot of us.",
    } },
    'Cross-Cut': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She leaned to one side, and that side of the hall was cut away. Her reflection cut a stripe on the other, in the glass. Ground was left at the far wall past the stripe, and in a narrow strip between the stripe and the cut. I took the strip.",
      gloomsworn: "She leaned toward one side and the whole of it was cut. Her reflection cut a stripe on the far side, in the glass. Two pockets were left: the narrow ground between, and the far wall beyond the stripe. I took the narrow one, and the others took the wall.",
      thresher: "She leans toward one side, dear, and that side of the hall is cut right through. Her reflection lays a stripe in the glass on the other side. Two pockets are left: the far edge past the stripe, or the narrow floor between. Pick one, and step in time.",
      bloodreaver: "She leaned and the whole side she leaned to was cut away, and her reflection cut a stripe on the far side besides! A cruel little floor. Two pockets were left: the strip between, and the far wall beyond the stripe. I took the strip. The others took the wall and complained.",
      runeguard: "Her stance names a side; that side of the hall is cut. Her reflection lays a stripe across the glass on the other. The ground left is two pockets: the strip between the stripe and the cut, and the far edge beyond the stripe. We stood in the strip.",
      gorgemaw: "Half the table was carved away, the half she leaned to, and her reflection carved a strip out of the other half in the glass. That left two bare patches: the thin slice between the cuts, and the rim beyond the strip. I took the slice. A mean portion.",
      oathbroker: "The keeper's stance names the side to be forfeited, and the whole of it is levied. Her reflection levies a stripe of the glass on the other side. Two parcels remain unencumbered: the strip between the two, and the far edge beyond the stripe. I occupied the first.",
      ironpalm: "She leaned and half the hall was cut. Her reflection cut a stripe on the far side in the glass. Two quiet places remained: between the cuts, and past the stripe at the far wall. We stood between. The cut fell on empty floor.",
      ranger: "Stance names a side. That side's cut, wall to near centre. Reflection cuts a stripe in the glass on the other side. Two pockets: narrow strip between, and the far edge past the stripe. Held the strip. Called the pockets for the others.",
      shadowblade: "She leaned, and the side she leaned to was gone, so I took the other, naturally. But her reflection drew a stripe across the glass there, which was rude. Two pockets were left: between the cuts, and the far edge beyond the stripe. I took the strip, and stayed on her back.",
      pyromancer: "She leaned, and the side she leaned toward was cut away, and then her reflection cut a stripe through the glass on the other side! Two pockets were left: the narrow strip between and the far edge beyond the stripe. I took the strip, and my spell was not even interrupted.",
      thermist: "She leans. That side of the hall is cut. Her reflection cuts a stripe on the other, in the glass. Two pockets: the strip between the cuts, and the far edge beyond the stripe. I was in the strip before the cast ended, panting, thrilled, and untouched.",
      driftblade: "She leaned, the side she leaned to was gone, and her reflection laid a stripe through the glass on the other, so I cut straight across the floor into the strip between, there before the cast was done. The far edge was the other pocket, and a long run.",
      alembist: "Observed: a lean to one side, which names the side cut in the hall, and a stripe of the glass cut on the other. Method: stand in a pocket, either the strip between the two or the far edge beyond the stripe. Read her stance first, then the glass.",
      threadweaver: "She leaned to one side and cut all of it, and her reflection cut a seam across the glass on the other. That left two pockets: the narrow strip between the cuts, and the far edge past the seam. I sent my stitches to the strip, and the shield kept her steady.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady leaned to one side, and that half of the stage was cut away; her understudy cut a stripe through the glass on the other. Two safe marks: the strip between the cuts, and the far wings past the stripe. The company stood in the strip. (The Doll chose the wings.)",
      cleric: "She leaned toward one side and cut all of it, and her reflection cut a stripe on the other, in the glass. Two pockets remained: the narrow strip between, and the far edge beyond the stripe. I led my friends into the strip, none too close to either cut.",
      grovekeeper: "She leaned like a tree in a gale, and the side she leaned to was cut right through; her reflection ploughed a strip across the glass on the other. Two pockets of untouched ground were left: the strip between, and the far edge past the furrow. We took the strip.",
      chronomancer: "She leans, and the side she leans toward is cut; her reflection cuts a stripe of the glass on the other. Two pockets remain: the strip between and the far edge beyond the stripe. I will have chosen the strip already; it was the near one. We stood in it.",
      tidecaller: "She leaned like a wave about to break, and the side she leaned to was cut through; her reflection cut a stripe in the glass on the other. The dry places were two: the strip between and the far shore beyond the stripe. We stood in the strip.",
      lanternkeeper: "She leaned, and that half of the hall was cut; her reflection cut a stripe on the other half, in the glass. Two corners stayed whole: the strip between the cuts, and the far edge past the stripe. I lit the strip and brought everyone to it.",
      kenseier: "She leans before she cuts, and the lean names the side. Her reflection cuts a stripe on the other, in the glass. Two places are left: the strip between the cuts and the far edge beyond the stripe. I stood in the strip, and the others took the strip or the edge.",
      houndmaster: "She leaned and half the hall went. Her double cut a stripe through the glass on the other half. Two safe spots: the strip between the cuts, and the far edge past the stripe. Put us in the strip, dogs tight at my knee. Shield-man's near it too.",
    } },
    'Silver Thread': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A chain showed over one of the others, and the line between them and their reflection became a blade. It locked a breath before it landed. The marked one cleared the stretch toward the wall and did not walk into the line, and the rest of us stayed out of it.",
      gloomsworn: "One of us was chained to their reflection, and the line between them turned to a blade. It locked, and it fell. The marked one chose a bare lane toward the wall, and I kept out of every lane. I have been tied to worse. Nobody stood in the line.",
      thresher: "A little chain over somebody's head, dear: the line to their reflection is going to be a blade. It locks a breath before it falls, so the marked one walks to a bare lane and stands still, and the rest of us stay out of the strip. A tidy line.",
      bloodreaver: "A chain went over one of my friends, and the line between them and their reflection became a blade! Anyone standing in the line got cut, and I'd have enjoyed the wound, but nobody did. The marked one found bare floor, and we kept off the line once it locked.",
      runeguard: "The sign is a chain over a head. The line from that hero to their reflection becomes a blade, which locks and then falls. The marked hero chooses a lane with no one in it, and the rest of us leave the line alone after the lock.",
      gorgemaw: "One guest was chained to their reflection, and the thread between them became a carving knife. It locked, and then it fell. The marked one stood where nobody sat between them and the glass, and the rest of us left their line alone.",
      oathbroker: "A chain names one party, and the line between that party and their reflection is charged as a blade. It is fixed at the lock and falls after. The named party must keep the stretch to the glass free of other parties, and the rest of us stay clear of it.",
      ironpalm: "A chain over one of us, and then the line from them to their reflection said one hard word. It locked, then fell. The marked one stood where nobody stood between them and the wall, and we all stayed off the line. It was a short sentence.",
      ranger: "Chain over one of us. The line from them to their reflection turns to a blade, locks, falls. Marked one stands where the lane to the wall is empty, and does not walk into it. Everyone else off the strip once it locks. Easy to read from the side.",
      shadowblade: "A chain over one of my companions, and the line between them and their reflection became a blade. A line of sight, made lethal. The marked one stood where the lane was empty, and we all stayed out of the strip once it locked. I admired the geometry.",
      pyromancer: "A chain showed over another head, and the line to their reflection turned into a blade, a straight bright stripe! It locked a breath before it fell. The marked one stood where nobody was between them and the wall, and I stayed well out of the strip.",
      thermist: "Chain over a head. The line from that hero to their reflection becomes a blade. It locks, then falls. Keep the lane empty, keep off the strip once it locks. It is over fast, if you are not standing in it. I was not, and I ran cool.",
      driftblade: "A chain over one of the others and the line to their reflection became a blade, a straight lane across the floor, and the marked one ran to somewhere with nothing between them and the wall, and the rest of us gave the lane a wide berth once it locked.",
      alembist: "Observed: a chain on one hero, and the line from them to their reflection becomes a blade. Method: the marked hero stands where the lane to the wall is empty; everyone else stays out of the strip after the lock. It locks first and falls after, so there is time.",
      threadweaver: "A chain went over one of my stitches, and the line between them and their reflection became a blade. A seam made sharp. It locked and then it fell. The marked one stood where the lane was empty, and the rest of us stayed off the thread.",
      marionettist: "A chain over one of the company, and the line between them and their understudy became a blade, a path across the stage that locked, and fell. The marked player took a bare lane, and the rest of us stayed off the strip. (A short, clean cut, and a good one.)",
      cleric: "A chain showed over one of my friends, and the line to their reflection became a blade. It locks before it falls. I asked them to stand where nobody was between them and the wall, and begged the rest to keep out of the strip.",
      grovekeeper: "A chain over one of my lot, and the line between them and their reflection became a blade, like a furrow ploughed in a breath. It locks, then it falls. The marked one stood where the furrow would run through empty ground, and the rest of us left the row alone.",
      chronomancer: "The chain showed over a head, and the line to their reflection became a blade, locked, fell. The marked one stood where the lane was empty, and I will have kept the rest of us out of the strip; I did. The lock comes first, so nobody is cut by surprise.",
      tidecaller: "A chain over one of us, and the line between them and their reflection became a blade, a straight channel through the floor. It locked, and it fell. The marked one stood where the channel ran through empty water, and the rest kept clear.",
      lanternkeeper: "A chain showed over one of my friends, and the line to their reflection became a blade. I picked up the lamp and set it at the edge, so the lane was lit and empty, and the rest of us stayed off it once it locked. The marked one walked clear.",
      kenseier: "A chain over one of us: the line to their reflection becomes a stroke, and it locks before it falls. That is the count. The marked one stands where the lane to the wall is bare, and the rest of us stay out of the strip once it locks.",
      houndmaster: "Chain over one of us. Line from them to their double turns into a blade, locks, falls. Marked one stood where nobody was between them and the wall. Rest of us stayed off the strip, dogs too. Like keeping the flock off a drover's road.",
    } },
    'Through the Glass': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two of the others were pulled through into the glass, the knights knelt, and nothing struck at us. I was not taken, so I stayed in the hall and kept the footing for the rest. The fight now was circles, two bodies apiece.",
      gloomsworn: "Two of us went through the glass, bodies and all, and the knights knelt in the hall. I was left behind, which I am used to. Nothing struck us. The work was only the circles that followed, and filling them in pairs.",
      thresher: "Two of us were taken through the glass, dear, quite politely, and the knights knelt. A hush came over the hall. No blows, only circles to be filled in pairs, and nobody should hurry. The ones inside the glass are as much on time as the ones outside.",
      bloodreaver: "Two of my friends were dragged through the glass and the knights knelt! Nobody was hitting anyone, which I found hard to forgive. I was left in the hall with a job: the circles that came next wanted two bodies each. Not a scar in it, but I did my share.",
      runeguard: "A flash of light, and two heroes are drawn through the glass; the knights kneel and strike no more. I was not taken. What remains is circles on the floor, each needing two bodies, one here and one there if need be. The wall is a matter of pairs.",
      gorgemaw: "Two of my companions were drawn through the glass like plates through a hatch, and the knights knelt. No cooking went on at all. The only work was the little rings that followed, each wanting two mouths. I stayed in the hall and waited my turn.",
      oathbroker: "Two parties were drawn through the glass and the knights suspended their performance. I was not conscripted. What followed was a schedule of circles, each requiring two bodies, whichever room they stood in. The wall is a joint obligation.",
      ironpalm: "The knights knelt and two of us were taken through the glass. No blows. A long silence, and then circles, each needing two. I stayed in the hall and waited. The ones in the glass saw the floor from the other side.",
      ranger: "Two of us pulled through the glass. Knights knelt, no cuts. Rest of the fight is circles that want two bodies each. I wasn't taken; stayed in the hall, counted heads, watched the floor. Two bodies in the glass, two in the hall.",
      shadowblade: "They knelt, and two of us were taken into the glass, not I, which stung. The hall went quiet, nothing struck, and the work became circles that each wanted two. I stayed in the hall, where the shadows are, and watched my friends across the wall.",
      pyromancer: "Two of us were pulled right through the glass, bodies and all, and the knights knelt in front of nobody! I was left in the hall. No blows, only circles that wanted two of us each, and I stood by for mine, annoyed at being so calm.",
      thermist: "Two of us dragged through the glass, knights kneeling. Nothing strikes. Just circles, two bodies each. I was left in the hall, pacing, quiet, then fidgeting. The wall is a long stillness with pairs in it, and I ran hot with waiting.",
      driftblade: "Two of us got pulled through the glass and I was not one, and the knights knelt and nothing struck and I had to stand and wait, which is the cruellest thing, for the circles that wanted two of us each, one body here, one there, and I wanted to run.",
      alembist: "Trial: two heroes pulled through the glass, knights kneeling, no blows. Result: the fight becomes circles that each need two bodies. Method: pairs; and a full shelf, since the wall is a long stretch without a pause.",
      threadweaver: "Two of my stitches were drawn through the glass and the knights knelt. Nothing struck at us. The work was circles that wanted two of us each, so I kept every stitch counted, in the hall and the glass alike, and mended them when they needed it.",
      marionettist: "A new scene: two of the company were drawn through the glass, and the knights knelt in the wings. No cuts, only circles that wanted two players each, one from each side of the glass if need be. I stayed in the hall and cued my side.",
      cleric: "Two of my friends were taken through the glass, and the knights knelt. Nothing struck. I stayed in the hall with whoever was left, and we waited for the circles, each of which wanted two. I kept my eyes on the ones in the glass.",
      grovekeeper: "Two of my lot were drawn through the glass, and the knights knelt. A hush, like the stillness before a thaw. The work was circles on the floor, each needing two. I stayed with the ones in the hall and watched the others across the wall.",
      chronomancer: "Two of us are drawn through the glass, which I will have seen coming; and the knights kneel. Nothing strikes. What follows is circles that want two bodies each, one from each room if that is how it falls. I stayed in the hall with the rest.",
      tidecaller: "Two of us were drawn through the glass like a pair of swimmers taken by a current, and the knights knelt. The hall went slack and quiet. What followed was circles, each wanting two. I stayed in the hall and watched the water across the glass.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two of us were taken through the glass, and the knights knelt. No cuts. I counted: two lamps in the glass, two in the hall. What remained was circles, each wanting two of us, and I stayed in the hall and kept a light on every one.",
      kenseier: "Two of us were taken through the glass, and the knights knelt: no stroke to meet, only a long rest. I was not taken. What followed was circles, each wanting two bodies, whichever room they stood in. I stood in the hall and kept the count.",
      houndmaster: "Two of us pulled through the glass. Knights knelt. No cuts at all. Dogs sat. Rest was circles on the floor that wanted two bodies each, one here and one there if need be. Stayed in the hall, watched the glass for our lot.",
    } },
    'Shared Reflection': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two blue circles, each wanting two bodies. One of each room made a pair: a hall hero in the circle, a pulled-through hero in the glass copy of it. Alone, the circle hurt every one of us. We filled both, round by round. Footing mattered.",
      gloomsworn: "Blue circles, each hungry for two. A body in the hall and a body in the glass count together, and the glass keeps its own copy of each. A circle with one in it hurts everybody. I walked into mine with the other hall hero, and we stood in it.",
      thresher: "Two blue circles, dear, each wanting a pair. A hero in the hall and a hero in the glass count together, and the ones in the glass take the copy of the circle that stands there. Walk in together and nobody is late.",
      bloodreaver: "Circles that wanted two bodies each, and if only one stood in it, EVERYONE paid! I took mine with a friend, both of us standing right in it. The ones in the glass walked into their own copies of the circles, and between us we were not short.",
      runeguard: "Each circle needs two bodies, in either room: the glass shows its own copy of each, and the pulled-through take it where they see it. A circle with fewer than two hurts all of us. We filled each in pairs, and the sides swapped from round to round.",
      gorgemaw: "Two blue plates, each meant for two mouths. One in the hall and one in the glass will do, and the glass sets down its own copy of each plate. A plate with one mouth on it served every one of us a bitter dish. We filled both.",
      oathbroker: "Each circle requires two bodies, and either room's will do, the glass holding its own copy. A circle short of its pair is a default, and the penalty falls on every party, not only the absent. We covered each obligation in full, in pairs.",
      ironpalm: "Two circles, each needing two. A body in the hall and a body in the glass answered it together, and the glass keeps its own copy of each. A circle with one in it spoke to all of us, harshly. We answered each in pairs.",
      ranger: "Two blue circles, two bodies each. Hall and glass count together. Glass has its own copy of each circle; the ones through take that one. One in a circle, everyone pays. Counted heads, split one and one. Sides swapped every round.",
      shadowblade: "Two circles, and each wanted a pair, which I like: one of me in the hall, one of someone else in the glass. The ones through take the glass's own copy. A circle with only one in it hurt everyone. Rude. The sides swapped each round, so I kept an eye out.",
      pyromancer: "Blue circles that wanted two of us each, one in the hall and one in the glass if need be! The ones through walked into the glass's own copy of the circle. One body alone and everybody paid. We did not make that mistake twice.",
      thermist: "Two blue circles, two bodies each. Hall and glass count together. The glass has its own copy of each, and the ones through walk to that. One body alone costs everyone. We paired off, and it went quiet, and my pulse caught up.",
      driftblade: "Two blue circles and each wanted two of us, one from the hall and one from the glass if we liked, so I ran to the nearest one in the hall and stood, and the one in the glass ran to their own copy of it, and between us the circle had its pair.",
      alembist: "Observed: blue circles, each wanting two bodies, in either room. Note: the glass holds its own copy of each circle, and the ones through take that one. A single body in a circle costs every one of us. Method: pair up, one and one or two together. Count before you step.",
      threadweaver: "Two circles, each needing two. A hero in the hall and a hero in the glass sew a circle up between them, and the one in the glass takes the glass's own copy. One alone and every thread in the party snagged. I tied my stitches in pairs, and the seams held.",
      marionettist: "Two marks on the floor, each wanting two players, hall or glass. The ones through hit the glass's own copy of the mark. One player alone and the whole company took the penalty. (We rehearsed in pairs, of course.) The marks swapped sides every round.",
      cleric: "Blue circles, each needing two. One in the hall and one in the glass count together, and the ones in the glass take the glass's own copy. A circle with one in it hurt every one of us. I helped my friends pair off.",
      grovekeeper: "Two blue rings, each wanting two. A body in the hall and a body in the glass counts, and the glass plants its own copy of each. One body alone and all of us paid. We paired up, and mended after.",
      chronomancer: "Two blue circles, and each wants two, hall or glass; the glass keeps its own copy, and the ones through step into that. A circle with only one in it will have hurt all of us; I saw to it that it did not. We paired off.",
      tidecaller: "Two blue pools, each needing two. A body in the hall and a body in the glass count together, and the glass keeps its own copy of each pool. One alone and the whole party took the penalty. We went in by twos, and waited for the last.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two blue circles, each needing two. One of us in the hall and one in the glass lights it together, and the glass has its own copy of each. A circle with one lamp in it hurt every one of us. I counted heads: two and two, a lamp in each ring.",
      kenseier: "Blue circles, each needing two bodies, either room. The glass keeps its own copy of each, and the pulled-through take it where it shows. A circle with one in it strikes every one of us, and the count is lost. We paired off, and stepped in on the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Two blue circles, two bodies each. Hall and glass count together, glass has its own copy of each, so the lads through walked to where it showed. One body in a ring and the whole lot of us paid. Paired off. Dogs sat out.",
    } },
    'The Mirror Turns': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Last circle filled; the hall stood still. Then she lifted the blade, a light blast came, and everyone was back on the hall floor. The mirror had left the north wall and stood against the east. Report: my reflection answers across the east wall now, so I stepped east and watched mine step west.",
      gloomsworn: "When the last circle was met the room went quiet, and I did not trust the quiet. A light blow, and we were back in the hall, the glass moved from the north wall to the east. A reflection that walks the other way is the least of what I have lived with.",
      thresher: "Everyone was still, dear, and then there was a small knock, and the mirror slid over from the north wall to the east and we were all home on the hall floor. Your reflection copies you sideways now: step east and it steps west. Quite a trick for an old wall.",
      bloodreaver: "We filled the last circle and she turned the mirror like a key! A light blast, and the wall of glass was the east wall, and every one of us was back on the real floor. My reflection mirrors east and west now. I tested it by charging at the wall.",
      runeguard: "After the last circle there is a pause, a light blast, and the mirror passes from the north wall to the east; all are returned to the hall. For the margin: every reflection now answers east for west, and every old danger returns on its side. Read the glass afresh before moving.",
      gorgemaw: "The last ring filled, the kitchen went silent, a light blast shook the pans, and the mirror moved from the north wall to the east. We were all back at the table. The same dishes came out turned on their sides. A reflection that mirrors east and west is a new seating plan.",
      oathbroker: "Upon the filling of the last circle the hall fell still, and a light levy followed: the mirror was removed from the north wall to the east, and all parties returned to the hall. The reflections now answer east for west. All earlier terms apply as written, but laterally.",
      ironpalm: "The last circle was filled and the whole hall held its breath. A light blow, and the mirror stood on the east wall and we stood on the hall floor again. Now my reflection answered across the east: I moved east, it moved west. The old words returned, said sideways.",
      ranger: "Last circle done. Quiet. Light hit. Mirror's on the east wall now, everyone back on the hall floor. Reflections mirror east to west. Every old move comes back sideways. Re-read before you step.",
      shadowblade: "When the last circle was filled the hall held its breath, which I always enjoy, and then a light blow moved the mirror from the north wall to the east and put us all on the hall floor. My shadow now mirrors east for west. I tried it out in a corner.",
      pyromancer: "We filled the last circle and the whole hall froze for one splendid moment! A light blast, and the mirror jumped from the north wall to the east, and everyone was home again. My reflection stepped west when I stepped east, and I tripped over my own feet to prove it.",
      thermist: "Last circle filled. Silence, sharp as ice. Then a light blast, and the mirror moved to the east wall and all of us were back in the hall. Reflections swap east and west now. I stumbled twice learning it, and flushed hot each time. Everything old comes back sideways.",
      driftblade: "The last circle was filled and everything stopped, which I can bear for only a moment, and then a light blast and the mirror had moved to the east wall and all of us were back in the hall, and a step east made my reflection step west, and I learned it fast.",
      alembist: "Observed: last circle filled, stillness, a light blast, then the mirror moves from the north wall to the east and everyone returns to the hall. Reflections now answer east for west. Method: re-read every old move sideways before moving. Label the shelf accordingly.",
      threadweaver: "The last circle filled, the hall went still, and a light blast moved the mirror off the north wall and onto the east. I counted my stitches back onto the hall floor, one by one. Reflections now run east for west, so every old seam has to be sewn sideways.",
      marionettist: "The last circle filled and the house went dark and still. A light blast, and the mirror was struck from the north wall and hung on the east, and the company was whole in the hall. East and west trade places in the glass now. (Same play, new blocking.)",
      cleric: "When the last circle was filled, the hall went very still. Then a light blast, and the mirror moved from the north wall to the east, and my friends were all in the hall again. Their reflections answered east for west after that. I asked them to look before they stepped.",
      grovekeeper: "The last circle filled and the hall went quiet as a field before frost. A light blast, and the mirror moved from the north wall to the east, and we were all back on the same floor. Reflections ran east for west after that. Same seed, turned a quarter.",
      chronomancer: "The last circle filled and everything held. Then a light blast, and the mirror moved to the east wall and we were all back in the hall. Reflections answer east for west now. Everything old will have come back sideways; it came back, and I knew where to stand.",
      tidecaller: "The last circle was filled and the hall went slack and quiet. Then a light swell, and the mirror moved from the north wall to the east, and the party stood together in the hall again. Reflections ran east for west after that. A new shore, the same sea.",
      lanternkeeper: "The last circle was filled and the hall went still. A light blast, and the mirror moved to the east wall, and we were back in the hall, all four of us in one room. Reflections ran east for west after that, so I lit the east wall first.",
      kenseier: "The last circle filled and the room held its rest. A light blast, and the mirror left the north wall for the east, and we were all in the hall. Reflections answer east for west. Every old form returns, mirrored the other way round. I stepped east, and watched mine step west.",
      houndmaster: "Last ring filled, hall went still. Light blast, mirror came off the north wall and onto the east. All back on the hall floor, dogs too. Reflections go east for west now. Everything we'd learned came round again sideways. Rust's double ran off the other way.",
    } },
    'Evenfall Dance': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A dance of cuts, hers and her reflection's in turn, each aimed at someone whose number showed overhead. Each landed sooner and wider than the last. The marked one stepped sideways out of their cut, and the rest of us kept out of the one drawn next. The last was both of them at once.",
      gloomsworn: "Cuts in turn, hers and her reflection's, each aimed at a head with a number over it. The marked one stepped sideways when theirs was drawn, and not before. I was never numbered, and I stayed out of the next outline. The last came from both knights after a pause, and it was wide.",
      thresher: "A waltz of cuts, dear, hers and then her reflection's, each aimed at whoever wears the number. The cuts come a little quicker and a little wider each time. Sidestep your own as it's drawn, and mind the outline of the next. The last is both of them at once, after a pause.",
      bloodreaver: "A dance of cuts, hers, then the glass knight's, each at a hero with a number over their head! Quicker and wider each time, my friends. The marked one sidestepped theirs. The last was both knights at the same target after a breath, and it was a big one, and I envied them.",
      runeguard: "The cuts come in turn, hers and the reflection's, each at the hero whose number shows; each is drawn before it lands, and each lands sooner and wider than the last. The marked hero steps aside as theirs is drawn. After a pause, both strike the same hero at once.",
      gorgemaw: "A tasting menu of cuts: hers, then the reflection's, each plated for a guest with a number overhead, each course quicker and wider than the last. The guest stepped aside from their own and the rest of us stayed off the next outline. The last was both cooks at once, aimed at one guest.",
      oathbroker: "A schedule of cuts, alternating between the keeper and her reflection, each against the party whose number shows. Each is posted before it falls, and each falls sooner and wider than the last. The party named steps aside; the rest avoid the next posting. The final instrument is joint, and wide.",
      ironpalm: "She spoke in sentences of cuts, hers and her reflection's in turn, each at the one whose number showed. Each came sooner and wider than the last. The marked one stepped aside. The rest of us stayed out of the next outline. The last was both voices at once.",
      ranger: "Cuts in turn, hers and the reflection's. Number over the head shows the target. Each one's drawn before it lands, quicker and wider than the last. Marked one steps sideways as it's drawn. Stay out of the next outline. Last cut's both at once, after a pause.",
      shadowblade: "A number appeared over a head and a cut was aimed at it, hers and then her reflection's, taking turns like fine dancers. Each came faster and wider. The numbered one stepped aside as theirs was drawn, and I stayed out of the next. The last was a duet, at one poor soul.",
      pyromancer: "A dance of cuts, hers and her reflection's in turn, each pointing at whoever wore a number, and every one quicker and wider than the last! I stepped sideways out of mine as it was drawn, and stayed off the next outline. The last was both of them at once, at one hero.",
      thermist: "Cuts in turn, hers, then the reflection's, each aimed at a number overhead. Step aside as yours is drawn, stay off the next outline. Each comes quicker and wider than the last, and my breath went with them, and the last is both knights at once, at one hero, after a pause.",
      driftblade: "A dance of cuts, hers and then her reflection's, each aimed at whoever had the number, and every one quicker and wider, so I slipped sideways out of mine as it was drawn, round the next outline, and the last was both knights at once, so I ran wider.",
      alembist: "Observed: cuts in turn, hers, then her reflection's, each aimed at a numbered hero, each quicker and wider. Method: step aside as yours is drawn, and keep off the next outline. Final dose: both knights at once at one hero, after a pause.",
      threadweaver: "A long seam of cuts, hers and her reflection's in turn, each aimed at a numbered stitch, each quicker and wider than the last. The numbered one stepped aside as theirs was drawn. The rest of us stayed out of the next outline. The last was both together, pointed at one of us.",
      marionettist: "A dance for two: hers, then her understudy's, each cut aimed at the player with the number over their head, and every one quicker and wider. Step aside as yours is drawn, and keep off the next one's outline. The finale is a duet at one poor player. (Applause, eventually.)",
      cleric: "Cuts in turn, hers and her reflection's, each at whoever had a number over their head, and each a little quicker and wider. I begged my friends to step aside as theirs was drawn, and to keep off the next outline. The last was both knights at once at one of them.",
      grovekeeper: "A dance of cuts, hers and her reflection's, taking turns, each at the one with a number overhead, each a little quicker and wider, like a storm building. The numbered one stepped aside as theirs was drawn. The last was both at once, at one of my lot.",
      chronomancer: "Cuts in turn, hers and then her reflection's, each at the number overhead, and each lands a little sooner than the last, so the tempo quickens. Step aside as yours is drawn, and keep off the next outline. The last is both together, after a breath.",
      tidecaller: "A swell of cuts, hers and her reflection's in turn, each aimed at the one with the number overhead, each a little quicker and wider than the last. The numbered one stepped aside as theirs was drawn. The last was both at once, at one of us, after a long pause.",
      lanternkeeper: "Cuts in turn, hers and then her reflection's, each aimed at a numbered hero, each quicker and wider than the last. I put a lamp where the numbered one would step, and they stepped there as theirs was drawn. The last was both knights at once, at one of us.",
      kenseier: "A dance of cuts, hers and the reflection's in turn, each at the hero with a number, each coming sooner and wider than the last. The count quickens. The numbered one steps aside as theirs is drawn, and the rest of us leave the next outline alone. The last is both at once.",
      houndmaster: "Cuts in turn, hers and her double's, each at a numbered hero, each quicker and wider than the last. Stepped out sideways when mine was drawn, and Rust and Ash and I stayed off the next outline. Last one's both of them at once, at one man, after a pause.",
    } },
    'The Line': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The line is mine to set. She planted her blade in the middle of the hall and the mirror became a line across the floor, square to wherever I stood at that instant. I picked my ground for the party and kept it. The others learned where their reflections stood.",
      gloomsworn: "She stopped in the middle and planted her blade, and the dark in the glass went out. Where I stood decided where the mirror lay. A sentry chooses his ground once. Our reflections stood on the floor with us after that, across the line, and her own stood opposite her.",
      thresher: "She walks to the middle, dear, and stands so very still, and the blade goes in. Wherever our shield is standing then is where the mirror will run: a line across the floor. After that everyone's reflection is their twin across it, and hers steps out to meet her.",
      bloodreaver: "She stalked to the middle and drove the blade into the floor, and the mirror became a line across the hall, laid wherever I happened to be standing! A nice thing to be trusted with. Everyone's reflection stood on the far side after, same distance, and hers stepped out to face her.",
      runeguard: "The line is set by the shield. When she plants the blade in the middle, the mirror stands square to the place where I am at that instant, and the glass goes dark. Thereafter each reflection stands the same distance beyond the line as its hero before it, and hers faces her.",
      gorgemaw: "She planted her blade in the middle of my kitchen, and the mirror lay across the floor wherever I stood at that instant. I picked a spot with some thought. Afterwards every reflection sat across the line, the same distance past as its owner before it, and hers sat opposite her.",
      oathbroker: "The shield fixes the venue: wherever I stood when she planted her blade, there the mirror lay, a line across the hall. I selected the ground with care. Thereafter each hero's reflection resides across it at an equal distance, and hers takes the opposite side.",
      ironpalm: "She stood in the middle without a word and planted the blade, and the mirror became a line, set by where I stood. I chose where. Our reflections stood across it after that, as far past as we were before, and hers stepped out of the glass to face her.",
      ranger: "She went to the middle, stopped, planted the blade. Mirror's a line now, square to wherever the shield stands that instant. Shield picks it. After that each reflection's across the line at its owner's distance. Hers steps out opposite.",
      shadowblade: "I was not the one who set it, which I regretted: the line fell wherever our shield stood when she planted the blade. After that my shadow lay across it at my own distance, and hers stepped out to meet her. A shadow on the same floor is much harder to ignore.",
      pyromancer: "The blade went down in the middle and the glass went dark, and the mirror became a line wherever our shield happened to be standing! A great honour for the shield. After that my reflection stood across it, at exactly my distance, in the same room as me, and I had to look at myself.",
      thermist: "Blade in the middle. Glass dark. A line across the floor, square to the shield. And then my reflection was in the room with me, across the line, the same distance past it as I stood before it, and it felt like standing next to my own breath.",
      driftblade: "She planted the blade in the middle and the mirror became a line, wherever the shield stood, and I ran my eye along it, and after that my reflection was across it from me, at my own distance, in the same room, and I could run toward it or away, whichever the floor allowed.",
      alembist: "Entry: the mirror is no longer a wall; it is a line across the hall, set by where the shield stood when she planted the blade. Observed: her reflection steps out opposite; each hero's lies across the line at the hero's own distance. The glass goes dark. A tidy hazard.",
      threadweaver: "She drove the blade into the floor and the mirror became a single seam across the hall, laid where our shield stood. After that my stitches' reflections lay across the seam, each as far past as its stitch was before, and hers stepped out to face her. The seam is the shield's to place.",
      marionettist: "A new set piece: our leading lady planted the blade at centre stage, and the mirror became a line across the boards, laid wherever our knight stood. The understudies came out into the same room, each across the line from its player. Hers stood opposite her. (The Doll chose her side.)",
      cleric: "Our knight chose where, though not by thinking about it: she planted the blade in the middle and the mirror lay across the floor wherever they were standing. After that every reflection stood in the room with us, across the line, and hers stepped out to face her.",
      grovekeeper: "She drove the blade into the middle of the floor like a stake into a field, and the mirror became a fence-line across it, laid where my big fellow stood. Our reflections stood on the far side of the fence, each the same distance from it as its owner, and hers stepped out opposite.",
      chronomancer: "She plants the blade in the middle, and the mirror lies across the floor wherever the shield is standing as it goes in. I will have asked them to stand somewhere sensible. After that, our reflections stood across the line in the same room, each as far past as we were before. Hers stepped out.",
      tidecaller: "She planted the blade in the middle and went still, and the mirror ran across the floor like a shoreline, wherever the shield was standing. Our reflections lay on the far side of it, as far past as we stood before, and hers stepped out of the glass onto the sand.",
      lanternkeeper: "I watched the shield, because the line followed them: she planted the blade in the middle and the mirror lay across the floor where they stood. I hung a lamp on the line. Our reflections stood across it after that, as far past as we stood before, and hers stepped out.",
      kenseier: "She plants the blade, and the mirror lies where I stood: a line across the floor, square to my place. That was my stroke to place, and I placed it with care. Our reflections stood across the line in the same room after that, and hers stepped out to meet her.",
      houndmaster: "Blade went in at the middle, and the mirror's a line across the floor, square to wherever the shield-man's standing. Shield-man picks. After that our doubles stand across the line, same distance as us, in the same room. Hers steps out and faces her. Rust sat on the line, daft lad.",
    } },
    'Last Reflection': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Her last cast filled the whole hall and no shield turns it. We had dodged well and struck too little, so she had time to finish. Next time every opening is used, and she is down before she lifts the blade.",
      gloomsworn: "She stopped waiting, and the whole hall, glass and all, went white. Nothing to hold against it. We had been too careful, and she had too much time. I have kept watch long enough to know when the watch is over. We should have hit her sooner.",
      thresher: "Her hour finally came for us instead, dear. The last cast filled the hall, glass and all, with no corner left to stand in. We had been too fussy, and she had time to finish. Next time, a stroke in every pause, and be done before she lifts the blade.",
      bloodreaver: "She ran out of patience and filled the whole hall with a last cast, glass and all! I had no wound to answer it with. We had dodged too much and struck too little. Next time harder and sooner, my friends, before she finishes.",
      runeguard: "The last cast was foretold at the margin of every act: when the fight runs long, she stops waiting. No ward turns it; only a faster party escapes it. Every opening must be struck, and she must fall before the cast does.",
      gorgemaw: "The last course arrived while I was still on the second, and it filled the whole hall. A slow table gets no dessert. We had dodged every dish and eaten too little of her. Next time, bigger bites, and her cleared before the last bell.",
      oathbroker: "The term expired, and the final instrument fell upon every party in both rooms. We had spent our time on avoidance and too little on performance. Next time, every opening struck in full, and the counterparty discharged before it falls.",
      ironpalm: "She stopped answering and spoke one last thing to all of us, and it could not be answered. We had danced too long and struck too little. Next time, a harder answer in every pause, and her finished before she speaks.",
      ranger: "Last cast hit the whole hall, glass and all. No safe corner. Too many careful steps, not enough arrows. Hit her whenever she's open. Down before she lifts the blade.",
      shadowblade: "She ran out of patience, which I took personally, and the last cast filled every corner of the hall. We had been too careful with the dodging. Strike her in every pause, and be done before she lifts the blade. Nothing slips out of that.",
      pyromancer: "She ran out of patience and the whole hall went white, glass and all! My spells had been too few and too shy, and I had been far too polite about the dodging. Burn harder, burn sooner, and have her down before she lifts the blade.",
      thermist: "Last cast, whole hall, glass and all. Late. Too many careful steps and too few strikes. I ran cold after it, and then I ran out. Hit hard in every opening, and finish early, and finish her with fire.",
      driftblade: "Her last cast filled the hall and there was nowhere to run, and I ran anyway, round and round the glass, because we had spent too long dodging and not long enough striking. Next time fewer laps, harder hits, and she is down before she lifts the blade.",
      alembist: "Observed: she ran out of patience, and the last cast reaches every corner of both rooms. Method: finish her sooner. Our dose came too slowly, and the openings were not spent in full. Label: this batch spoils on schedule.",
      threadweaver: "Her last cast cut every thread at once, glass and all. We had been too slow and too tidy with our dodging. Next time, hit her harder in every opening, and finish before she lifts the blade.",
      marionettist: "The last cast brought down the whole house, glass and all. We had rehearsed the dodging beautifully and the killing far too little. Strike in every pause, and have her down before the curtain. (The critics were unkind, and correct.)",
      cleric: "Her last cast filled the hall, glass and all, and not even I could shield every one of us from it. I had mended too much and urged too little. Next time I will tell them to strike harder, and to finish her early.",
      grovekeeper: "Her last cast came down like a hard frost over the whole hall, glass and all, and took every one of my lot. We had dithered, and the season ran out. Next time, strike hard in every pause and have her down before she lifts the blade.",
      chronomancer: "Her last cast came at the same hour, however often I undid it. We had been too slow, and too many openings went half used. Next time, hit harder and finish her before she lifts the blade. I will have said that before.",
      tidecaller: "The last cast came in like a flood that does not turn, and the hall and the glass went under together. We had lingered over the dodging while the water rose. Next time, quicker, and harder in every gap, and her finished before she lifts the blade.",
      lanternkeeper: "Her last cast put out every lamp in the hall at once, glass and all. We had spent too many wicks on careful dodging and too few on striking. Next time, hit her in every pause, and have her down while the oil lasts.",
      kenseier: "She stopped waiting, and the last cast filled the hall, a stroke with no beat to meet and no edge to turn. We had been slow in the openings. Next time, a harder stroke in every pause, and her finished before the blade is lifted.",
      houndmaster: "Last cast took the whole hall, glass and all, and the lot of us with it. Took too long on the dodging, not enough on the hitting. Next time, hit her hard whenever she's open, and have her finished before she lifts the blade.",
    } },
    'Double Glass': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "She laid the glass and the hall at once. Shards fell in the glass on the spots where some of us had stood, and a row of rings crossed the hall floor. I read both floors. The ones who stepped off their spot, and not into the row, took nothing.",
      gloomsworn: "Two floors, two patterns, one cast. In the glass the shards fell where some of us had stood; in the hall a row of rings crossed the floor. I stood where neither fell. I did not stand in the gaps between the rings either. They are narrow.",
      thresher: "She laid both floors at once, dear: shards in the glass where a few of us had stood, and a row of rings across the hall. Step off your spot, and find open ground north or south of the row, not in the gaps. Both land together, so no dawdling.",
      bloodreaver: "Two floors at once! Shards in the glass where a few of us had stood, and a row of rings across the hall, and both landed together. The gaps looked big until I was standing in one. I kept clear of the row and off the old spots, and took my wounds elsewhere.",
      runeguard: "Two patterns are laid together: shards in the glass on the spots where some of us had stood, and in the hall a row of rings across the floor. Leave the spot, read the glass for the stray, and stand clear of the row, not in the gaps between.",
      gorgemaw: "Two dishes at once: the glass served shards on the spots where some of us were seated, and the hall served a row of rings across the floor. Leave your seat, and keep off the row. The gaps between the rings are only for the thin.",
      oathbroker: "Two instruments executed at once: in the glass, shards upon the places where some of us stood; in the hall, a row of rings across the floor. Vacate the spot, and do not seek shelter in the gaps between the rings, since the margins are thin.",
      ironpalm: "Two things said at once, one in each room. Shards in the glass, on the spots where some of us had stood. A row of rings across the hall. I read both. The ones who stepped off their spot, and not into the row, were not touched.",
      ranger: "Two patterns at once. Glass: shards where some of us stood. Hall: a row of rings across the floor. Step off the old spot, then stay clear of the row. Gaps between rings are tight. North or south of the row is better.",
      shadowblade: "Both floors were set at once, which is greedy. The glass had its shards on the spots where some of us had stood, and the hall had a row of rings. I slipped off my spot and went round the row, not between it. Never trust a narrow gap.",
      pyromancer: "Both floors at once! Shards in the glass where some of us had been standing, and a row of rings across the hall, all landing together. I stepped off my spot, and not into the row, and the gaps in the row are for the brave and foolish. I was neither.",
      thermist: "Two floors, one cast. Shards in the glass on the spots where some stood; a row of rings across the hall. Step off your spot, stay out of the row. The gaps look wide and aren't, and I learned that hot-footed. Go north or south of it.",
      driftblade: "Two floors at once, shards in the glass on the spots where some of us had stood, and a row of rings across the hall, so I left my spot early and ran round the end of the row, not through the gaps, which are tighter than they look, and was clear of both.",
      alembist: "Observed: two patterns at once. Glass: shards on the spots where some stood. Hall: a row of rings. Method: leave the spot, read the glass for the stray, keep clear of the row. The gaps between the rings are narrow, and do not count as clear.",
      threadweaver: "She laid the glass and the hall at once: shards in the glass on the spots where some of us stood, and a row of rings across the floor. I sent my stitches off their spots and clear of the row, round it, not between. The gaps are narrower than they look.",
      marionettist: "Two stages lit at once: shards in the glass on the marks where some of the company stood, and a row of rings across the hall. Leave your mark, and keep off the row. (The gaps between the rings are not an entrance.)",
      cleric: "She laid both floors together: shards in the glass on the places where some of my friends had stood, and a row of rings across the hall. I called them off their spots and clear of the row, not into its gaps, which are narrow. They came through whole.",
      grovekeeper: "Hail in the glass on the spots where some of my lot had stood, and a row of rings across the hall, both landing together. I moved them off their spots and round the row, not through its gaps; they are narrow, like a gate left on the latch.",
      chronomancer: "She lays both floors together: shards in the glass on the spots where some of us stood, and a row of rings across the hall. I will have moved off mine already, and gone round the row, not between. The gaps are narrower than they look.",
      tidecaller: "Two shores at once: shards in the glass on the spots where some of us stood, and a row of rings across the hall. We left the spots and kept clear of the row, wide of it, not through the gaps, which are narrow as channels at low water.",
      lanternkeeper: "She laid both floors together: shards in the glass on the spots where some of us stood, and a row of rings across the hall. I lit a bare patch beside the row and sent my friends to it, off their spots and clear of the gaps.",
      kenseier: "Two strokes laid at once, one in each room: shards in the glass on the spots where some of us stood, and a row of rings across the hall. We left our spots and kept clear of the row, not between its rings. The gaps are narrow. A blade does not fit them either.",
      houndmaster: "Both floors at once. Glass had shards where some of us had stood, hall had a row of rings across it. Moved off my spot early, went round the end of the row, dogs with me. Gaps between the rings are too tight for two dogs and a man.",
    } },
    'Two Threads': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Two of the others wore chains, a one and a two. Both blades came down together, so the numbers were names and not an order. Each walker took bare ground, far from the other. I stood off both lanes.",
      gloomsworn: "Two of us were chained, a one and a two, and I was neither. Both blades land at once, so the numbers are only names. They stood a long way apart, each in an empty lane. I kept out of both.",
      thresher: "Two small chains, dear, wearing a one and a two like name tags. Both blades fall at the same moment, so there's no queue. The pair stand well apart, each with an empty lane to the wall, and the rest of us stay out of both.",
      bloodreaver: "Two friends got chains, a one and a two, and I felt jealous. Both blades dropped together, so the numbers were only names. They went opposite ways, each to a bare lane. I stayed out of both strips, and kept my wounds for something worth having.",
      runeguard: "Two heroes are chained and numbered, and the numbers are labels, not a sequence: both blades lock and fall together. Each walks to a lane that is empty, and neither enters the other's. We who are not marked stay out of both strips, which is the whole of the instruction.",
      gorgemaw: "Two guests wore chains, labelled one and two like courses, but both knives came down together; there was no order of service. The pair sat far apart, each with a clear lane, neither in the other's way. The rest of us ate elsewhere.",
      oathbroker: "Two named parties, designated one and two, though the numbers confer no priority: both blades take effect at once. The parties keep apart, each with a clear lane, and neither stands in the other's. All other parties remain outside both lines.",
      ironpalm: "Two chains, two blades, and one beat for both: they landed together. A one and a two hung over the pair, but only to tell them apart. They stood far from each other, in empty lanes. We left both lanes empty.",
      ranger: "Two marked, numbered one and two. Numbers aren't order; both blades drop on the same beat. Pull them apart: opposite lanes, nobody between either of them and the wall. Rest of us off both strips.",
      shadowblade: "A one and a two over two heads, which sounds like precedence and isn't; both blades fell together. They kept to opposite lanes, each with an empty path to the wall. I took no part in either, and drifted well out of both.",
      pyromancer: "Two chains, a one and a two, and I was passed over twice! Both blades fell at once, so there was no queue. The two went to opposite lanes, each empty to the wall. I watched from outside both and tried not to look envious.",
      thermist: "Two chains, numbered. The numbers mean nothing; both blades land together. Opposite lanes, then. Empty all the way to the wall. And the rest of us out of both, quick, and sweating a little.",
      driftblade: "Two chains and the numbers meant nothing, both blades coming down together, so the pair split away to opposite lanes with nothing between them and the wall, and I curved round the outside of both, never in a strip, and still found a gap to run.",
      alembist: "Observed: two chains, numbered, one reaction: both blades fire together. The numbers are labels. Method: each marked hero to an empty lane, well away from the other. Anyone not chained stays out of both strips. Easier than it sounds.",
      threadweaver: "Two of my stitches had chains, labelled one and two, and I wanted it understood that the labels meant nothing: both blades fell together. I sent them to opposite lanes, each empty to the wall, and told everyone else to keep out of both.",
      marionettist: "Two names in lights, a one and a two, though no billing order: both blades land together. The pair took opposite wings, each lane clear to the wall. The rest of the company stayed out of both strips. (The Doll was spared, as ever.)",
      cleric: "Two of my friends wore chains, and I told them the numbers were only names: both blades fall at once. I walked them to opposite sides, each with a clear lane, and kept the rest of us clear of both strips.",
      grovekeeper: "Two rows to plough, marked one and two, but there's no waiting your turn: both blades come down together. Put the pair at opposite ends of the field, each lane empty to the wall. The others kept off both rows.",
      chronomancer: "Two chains, so two blades, and the numbers are a trick: nothing is first, everything lands together. I sent the pair to opposite lanes, each empty to the wall, and kept everyone else out of both. The echo never came.",
      tidecaller: "Two chains, one and two, and both blades broke at once like two waves on the same beat. The numbers only name them. Opposite lanes, each empty to the shore, and the rest of us kept out of both.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two chains, a one and a two. I set a lamp at opposite edges of the hall and sent each marked friend to theirs. Both blades fall together, so the numbers are only names. The rest of us stayed off both strips.",
      kenseier: "Two chains, one count: both strokes fall on the same beat, and the numbers only name the two who wear them. They stand apart, each in a lane bare to the wall. The rest of us keep out of both.",
      houndmaster: "Two of ours chained, one and two on their heads, but both blades come down together so it's only names. Sent them to opposite ends, lanes clear to the wall. The rest of us and the dogs stayed out of both strips.",
    } },
    'Hand Mirror': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "She threw a standing mirror into the hall. Anyone near it had a third body across it, struck by everything that fell. I kept out of its strip, and the Evenfall behind it fell only on the two of me I knew. When it broke, I was well clear of its middle.",
      gloomsworn: "The Glass Knight threw a mirror into the hall and left it standing. Whoever stood close to it grew a third body, on the far side, and everything that fell struck that one too. I stayed out of its reach, and out of the ring at its middle when it broke.",
      thresher: "A little standing mirror, dear, thrown into the hall, and anyone near it grows a third self across it, and everything that falls finds that one as well. Stay out of its reach, and clear of its middle when it breaks. One reflection is plenty to keep track of.",
      bloodreaver: "She threw a mirror into the hall and left it standing, and anyone near it grew a third body! Every blow would have found that one as well, and I like my wounds counted once. I kept out of its reach, and out of the ring at its middle when it broke.",
      runeguard: "A mirror thrown into the hall is a standing sign: anyone within its reach has a third body, set across it, and all that falls strikes that one as well. The wise stay outside its reach, and clear of its middle when it shatters in a ring.",
      gorgemaw: "A mirror was set down in the hall like a serving tray, and whoever stood near it got a third portion of themselves on the other side, which every dish landed on as well. I stayed well away, and left its middle alone when it broke.",
      oathbroker: "A mirror was posted in the hall as a standing clause: whoever stands near it acquires a third body across it, which every instrument strikes as well. The remedy is to stand outside its reach, and clear of its middle when it breaks in a ring.",
      ironpalm: "She threw a mirror into the hall and it stood. Anyone near it had a third body across it, and whatever fell struck that one too. I stayed away from it, and from the ring at its middle when it broke. One reflection was already enough.",
      ranger: "Mirror thrown into the hall, stands there. Anyone near it grows a third body across it, hit by everything. Stay out of its reach. A plain Evenfall came behind it. Clear of its middle when it breaks, too. Wide strip, easy to see.",
      shadowblade: "She threw a mirror into the hall, and anyone near it grew a third shadow across it, struck by everything the hall laid, which is overkill. I stayed out of its reach, and away from its middle when it burst. One shadow is enough for a lifetime.",
      pyromancer: "She threw a mirror right into the hall, and anyone near it grew a third body on its far side! Everything that fell found that one too. I stayed out of its reach and finished my spell from a safe distance. The ring when it broke was brighter than my best.",
      thermist: "Mirror thrown into the hall, stands there. Anyone near it grows a third body across it, struck by everything. Stay out of its reach. The ring at its middle when it breaks: out of that too. A plain Evenfall is laid behind it, and I watched it over my shoulder, hot with caution.",
      driftblade: "A mirror thrown into the hall and left standing, and anyone near it grew a third body on the far side, hit by everything that fell, so I stayed wide of its reach, and round it, and well away from the ring at its middle when it broke.",
      alembist: "Observed: a standing mirror thrown into the hall. Anyone near it has a third body across it, struck by everything. Method: stay out of its reach, and clear of its middle when it breaks in a ring. A plain Evenfall follows, and finds only the usual two.",
      threadweaver: "A standing mirror was thrown into the hall, and anyone near it grew a third body across it, caught by everything like a loose thread. I sent my stitches out of its reach, and clear of its middle when it burst in a ring.",
      marionettist: "A standing mirror was thrown on stage, and anyone near it grew a third player on the far side, struck by everything laid. The company stayed out of its reach, and clear of its middle when it burst in a ring. (The Doll, naturally, was in the wings.)",
      cleric: "She threw a mirror into the hall, and anyone near it grew a third body across it, which everything struck. I called my friends out of its reach and clear of its middle, where it broke in a ring. There was only so much of them I could mend.",
      grovekeeper: "A mirror stood in the hall like a scarecrow, and anyone near it grew a third body across it, struck by everything. My lot kept out of its reach, and out of the ring at its middle when it broke. A plain sweep followed it, and found only two bodies each.",
      chronomancer: "A mirror is thrown into the hall and left to stand; anyone near it will have a third body across it, struck by everything. We stayed out of its reach and clear of its middle when it broke. A plain Evenfall followed it, and found only the usual two.",
      tidecaller: "A mirror was thrown into the hall and left standing like a still pool, and anyone near it grew a third body across it, struck by everything. We stayed clear of its reach, and of the ring at its middle when it broke.",
      lanternkeeper: "A mirror was thrown into the hall and left standing. Anyone near it had a third body across it, and every light struck that one as well. I kept us out of its reach and clear of its middle when it broke in a ring, and a plain Evenfall followed, easy to read.",
      kenseier: "A mirror is thrown into the hall and stands. Whoever is near it has a third body across it, struck by every stroke. We stood clear of its reach, and of the ring at its middle when it broke. The Evenfall behind it was plain, and honest.",
      houndmaster: "She chucked a mirror into the hall and it stood there. Anyone close to it grew a third body across it, hit by everything. Kept the lads out of its reach, and clear of the ring at its middle when it broke. Rust sat and barked at it anyway.",
    } },
    'Backhand': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "She leaned one way, and a breath before it landed the cut was gone and she swung the other way. I had not committed to the far wall. The middle of the floor was clear through both, and I stood in it. Those at the edge had a long walk.",
      gloomsworn: "She leaned toward one side, and at the last moment turned her blade and cut the other. A late change is the only kind a keeper trusts. The strip down the middle was clear of both cuts, so I took it early. The ones at the far wall had to cross.",
      thresher: "She leans one way, dear, and then changes her mind at the last moment and cuts the other. The middle of the floor is clear on either side, so the tidy choice is to wait there, not at the far edge. If you did go far, walk across as soon as she swings.",
      bloodreaver: "She leaned one way, then spun and cut the other, the little cheat! The first cut shown was a lie. The strip down the middle was clear both ways, so I waited there. Some of my friends went to the far wall and had to run across, grumbling.",
      runeguard: "Her stance names a side; a breath before it lands the cut is struck out and the other side drawn, and her stance swings across. The first drawing is false. The middle of the hall is clear of both, and the far edge must be crossed once the stance swings.",
      gorgemaw: "Half the table was marked for carving, and at the last moment the knife swapped sides and she carved the other half. A cheat's trick. The strip down the middle was bare both ways, so I sat there. The guests at the far rim had to run across.",
      oathbroker: "The first notice is withdrawn at the last moment and the opposite side substituted, and her stance swings across to confirm it. The first drawing binds nothing. The strip down the middle is clear of both, and a party at the far edge must cross promptly.",
      ironpalm: "She leaned and the first cut was drawn. Then, late, she turned and drew the other. The first had been a lie. The strip down the middle was clear of both, and I stood in it. Those who waited at the far wall crossed when her stance swung.",
      ranger: "The stance leans to a side. Cut's drawn. Then late, it's struck out and the other side drawn, stance swings across. First drawing's a feint. Middle strip clear of both, though thin once the mirror's on the east wall. Far edge means crossing the whole floor.",
      shadowblade: "She cut one side, then changed her mind a breath before it landed and cut the other, which is only good manners when it is done to someone else. The middle strip was clear of both. I stood there and watched the others run across.",
      pyromancer: "She leaned one way, and then at the very last instant she changed her mind and cut the other! The first drawing was only a lie. The middle strip is clear of both, so I stood there and was not even tempted by the edge. The edge people had to run.",
      thermist: "She leans, a cut is drawn, then it's struck out and the other side drawn. The first was a feint. The strip down the middle is clear of both. The far edge means a long sprint across, and I watched someone do it, gasping on their behalf. I stood in the middle.",
      driftblade: "She leaned one way and the cut was drawn and then she swung the other way and drew it again, a breath before it landed, so the strip down the middle, clear of both, was where I stood, and the ones out at the edge had to cross the whole floor with me watching.",
      alembist: "Observed: a Cross-Cut that changes sides a breath before it lands, her stance swinging across. The first drawing is a feint. Method: stand in the strip down the middle, clear of both; or cross the moment her stance swings. Never commit to the first reading.",
      threadweaver: "She drew one cut, then unpicked it a breath before it fell and sewed the other side, her stance swinging across. The first seam was a feint. The strip down the middle was clear of both, so my stitches waited there. The ones at the edge had a long way to run.",
      marionettist: "A late change in the blocking: the first cut was drawn, then struck out a breath before it landed, and the other side drawn, her stance swinging across. The first scene was a feint. The strip down the middle was safe both ways. (Edge players had to dash.)",
      cleric: "She leaned one way, and a breath before it landed she struck out that cut and drew the other, her stance swinging across. The first drawing was false. The strip down the middle was clear of both, so I kept my friends there. The ones at the edge had to hurry.",
      grovekeeper: "She leaned one way like a tree in a gale, then swung back a breath before it fell and cut the other. The first drawing was a feint. The strip down the middle was clear of both, and my lot waited there. The edge folk had a long dash.",
      chronomancer: "She leans, the cut is drawn, and then it is struck out and the other side drawn, a breath before it lands; I will have been in the middle already. The first drawing is a lie. The strip down the middle is clear of both. The edge is the long walk.",
      tidecaller: "The tide turned a breath before it landed: the first cut was struck out and the other side drawn, and her stance swung across. The first drawing was false. The strip down the middle was clear of both, and we waited there. The edge was a long way across.",
      lanternkeeper: "She drew one cut, then struck it out a breath before it landed and drew the other side, her stance swinging across. I had lit the strip down the middle, which was clear of both, and my friends waited there. The ones at the edge had a long dash to the other side.",
      kenseier: "She leans and a cut is drawn; then, a breath before the landing, it is struck out and the other side drawn, and her stance swings across. A feint with a late count. The strip down the middle is clear of both. The far edge has to be crossed.",
      houndmaster: "She leaned one way, drew the cut, then swung the other a breath before it landed. First cut was a feint. Middle strip's clear of both, so we stood there, dogs and all. Anyone out at the edge had to run the whole floor.",
    } },
    'Through the Glass Again': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Midway through the wall the glass changed its mind: a mark went over one of the others who had been left in the hall, and one of those pulled through was let go. I made sure the pairs were made again before the last round, each hero to the room they were in now.",
      gloomsworn: "Halfway through, the glass let go of one of the two it held and took one who had been left in the hall. A mark showed over the one it took. I stayed where I was. The pairs for the last round had to be made again, new ones in the new rooms.",
      thresher: "Halfway through the waiting, dear, the glass trades: a mark goes over one who was left in the hall, and one of the pair in the glass is sent home. Quite a good trick. So pair up afresh before the last round, new ones to their new rooms.",
      bloodreaver: "Halfway through, the glass swapped them! A mark went up over somebody left in the hall, and one of the two in the glass was thrown back. I like a good swap. We paired up again, new ones for the new rooms, before the last circles.",
      runeguard: "In the middle of the wall the glass exchanges one of its two for one who was left: a mark shows over the one to be taken, and one of the held is returned. The pairs must be reformed, each hero to their new room, before the last round.",
      gorgemaw: "Halfway through the course the plates were swapped: the glass handed one of its guests back and took one who had been left at the table, a mark shown over the new one. I rearranged the pairs before the last round, each to the room they now sat in.",
      oathbroker: "At the midpoint the glass exchanges one party it holds for one who remained in the hall, a mark appearing over the party to be taken. The pairings must be reconstituted before the final round, each hero within the room they now occupy.",
      ironpalm: "The glass spoke again halfway through. A mark showed over one who had been left, and one of the held was let go. We changed places in our minds, and the pairs were made again before the last round. The glass is not a quiet teacher.",
      ranger: "Mid-wall the glass swaps. Mark shows over one who was left in the hall, and one of the two in the glass is sent back. New pairs before the last round: new ones in new rooms. Watched the marks, called out who went where.",
      shadowblade: "Midway, the glass swapped a pair. A mark appeared over someone left behind in the hall, and one of those in the glass came back. I was rather glad to be watching. New pairs for the last round, with each hero in their new room.",
      pyromancer: "Halfway through, the glass traded! A mark popped over somebody left in the hall, and one of the two in the glass came home. We reformed the pairs before the last round, each hero in their new room. I had only just made friends with my circle.",
      thermist: "Halfway through, the glass trades one held hero for one in the hall, a mark over the one taken. It is quick. The pairs must be reformed at once, new ones to their new rooms, before the last round, and I ran hot with the hurry of it.",
      driftblade: "Halfway through the glass swapped them, a mark over somebody who had been left in the hall and one of the two in the glass sent home, so we rearranged ourselves, round and across, new ones to their new rooms, before the last round and I did not stand still once.",
      alembist: "Observed: midway, the glass exchanges one held hero for one who was left in the hall, a mark shown over the one taken. Result: the old pairs no longer work. Method: reform the pairs before the last round, each hero for their new room.",
      threadweaver: "Halfway through the wall the glass pulled one stitch out and put another in: a mark over somebody left in the hall, and one of the two held sent home. I retied the pairs before the last round, each hero to their new room, and checked the seams.",
      marionettist: "A mid-act substitution: a mark over someone left in the hall, and one of the two held in the glass sent back. New casting for the last round. The pairs reformed, each player in their new room. (The Doll was spared, as always.)",
      cleric: "Halfway through, the glass let one of the two go and took another who had been left in the hall, a mark showing over the one it would take. I helped my friends make new pairs before the last round, each in their new room, and kept a light on the one newly taken.",
      grovekeeper: "Halfway through the glass traded one of its two for one I'd left in the hall, a mark over the new one. A change of crop. We reformed the pairs before the last round, each hero to their new room.",
      chronomancer: "Halfway through, the glass trades: a mark over someone left in the hall, and one of the held sent home. I will have seen who was next; I did. The pairs reformed before the last round, each to their new room.",
      tidecaller: "Halfway through, the glass let one of the two go and took another who had been left in the hall, as a current takes a swimmer. A mark showed over the one taken. We reformed the pairs before the last round, each to the room they were in.",
      lanternkeeper: "Halfway through, a mark appeared over someone left in the hall, and one of those held in the glass was sent home. I carried a lamp across to match. We made new pairs before the last round, each hero to the room they were now in.",
      kenseier: "Halfway through, the glass exchanges one it holds for one left in the hall, a mark over the one it takes. A change of beat. We reformed the pairs before the last round, each hero to the room they now stood in, and found the new count.",
      houndmaster: "Halfway through the glass swapped. Mark went over a lad left in the hall, and one of the two in the glass came home. Had to pair up again before the last round, new ones in new rooms. Dogs sat tight.",
    } },
    'Lagging Glass': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A pale mark went over two of the others, and for a while their reflections trailed a step behind them. Anything laid in the glass struck the place where they had just been. They stood still before it landed, and the reflection caught up. I kept to my post.",
      gloomsworn: "Two of us wore a pale mark, and our reflections lagged behind us, standing where we had been. A glass danger lands there, not where we are. I stood still, and the reflection caught up. Standing still is a skill the dead have.",
      thresher: "Two of us wear a pale mark, dear, and their reflections run a little late. Glass dangers fall where you were, not where you are. So be in a safe spot a moment before it lands, and stand still, and your reflection catches up.",
      bloodreaver: "Two of my friends got a pale mark, and their reflections ran behind them, so the glass hit the place they had just left! The cure was to be somewhere safe early, then stand still while the reflection caught up. I like a trailing wound. Not this one.",
      runeguard: "A pale mark on two heroes: their reflections trail, standing where the hero stood a moment before. A danger in the glass strikes that old place. Those marked must be clear of the glass danger early, and stand still until the reflection catches up.",
      gorgemaw: "Two guests had a pale mark, and their reflections lagged at the table, still sitting where they had been a moment before. Glass dishes landed on the old seat. They stood up early, found a safe place, and sat still until the reflections caught up.",
      oathbroker: "Two parties are marked pale, and their reflections are delayed, bearing the place the party occupied a moment ago. Glass liabilities attach to that place. The marked must vacate early, then remain still, so that the reflection is brought into line.",
      ironpalm: "A pale mark on two of us, and their reflections trailed behind, standing where they had been. Glass dangers fell on the old place. They moved early and then were still, and the reflection caught up. I watched and said nothing.",
      ranger: "Pale mark on two. Their reflections lag, stand where they were a moment ago. Glass dangers land on the old spot. Clear it early, then stand still till the reflection catches up. Hall dangers still hit where you stand.",
      shadowblade: "A pale mark on two of us, and the reflection ran late, standing where its owner had just been, like a bad shadow. Glass dangers fell on the old place. I was somewhere safe early, and stood still until it caught up. Hall dangers still hit the body.",
      pyromancer: "Two of us got a pale mark, and their reflections dawdled behind, standing where they had been a moment before! Glass dangers fell on the old place. We got to a safe spot early, and stood still so the reflection could catch up. Stillness, my favourite.",
      thermist: "Pale mark on two. Reflections trailing, standing where the hero was a moment ago. Glass dangers land there. Move early, then freeze, so the reflection catches up. Hall dangers still strike the body where it stands now, and I ran cold with the effort of waiting.",
      driftblade: "A pale mark on two of us and their reflections trailed, standing where they'd just been, so a glass danger hit the old place, and I had to leave early and then, which I hate, stand still for a breath so the reflection could catch up, while the hall dangers only cared about me.",
      alembist: "Observed: a pale mark on two; their reflections stand where they were a moment ago. Result: glass dangers strike the old place. Method: reach safe ground early, then stand still until the reflection catches up. Hall dangers still strike the body as usual.",
      threadweaver: "A pale mark on two of my stitches, and their reflections trailed on a slack thread, standing where they had been. Glass dangers fell on the old place. I told them to settle early and stand still until the reflection came up, and mended the one who didn't.",
      marionettist: "Two players got a pale mark, and their understudies ran late, standing where their players had just been. Glass dangers fell on the old marks. They hit their safe mark early and held still until the understudy caught up. (Hall dangers still cared about the player.)",
      cleric: "Two of my friends bore a pale mark, and their reflections lagged, standing where they had been. Glass dangers fell on the old place. I asked them to move to safety early and stand still, so the reflection could catch up. The hall's dangers still struck where they stood.",
      grovekeeper: "A pale mark on two of my lot, and their reflections lagged behind like a seedling after the sun. Glass dangers landed on the old place. They found safe ground early and stood still until the reflection caught up. Hall dangers still struck the body.",
      chronomancer: "Two of us got a pale mark, and the reflection ran late, standing where the hero stood a moment before: I know that feeling. Glass dangers land on the old place. Be somewhere safe early, stand still, and it will have caught up. The hall dangers never lagged.",
      tidecaller: "Two of us bore a pale mark, and their reflections trailed behind like a wake, standing where they had just been. Glass dangers fell on the old water. We found safe ground early and stood still until the wake caught up. Hall dangers still struck the body.",
      lanternkeeper: "A pale mark went over two of us, and their reflections trailed behind, still standing where the lamp had been a moment before. Glass dangers fell on the old place. They reached safe ground early and stood still until the light caught up.",
      kenseier: "Two bear a pale mark, and their reflections lag a beat, standing where they stood before. Glass dangers fall on the old place. The marked reach safe ground early and stand still until the reflection catches up. Hall dangers still strike the body where it is.",
      houndmaster: "Pale mark on two lads, and their doubles ran behind, standing where they'd just been. Glass dangers hit the old spot. Got to safe ground early and stood still till the double caught up. Hall dangers still hit the man. Rust got confused.",
    } },
    'Wrong Hand': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Two of the others carried a warm mark, and their reflections stood far across the floor, corner to corner, not behind the wall. Glass dangers struck that far place. They chose ground that was safe where they stood, and clear across the middle too.",
      gloomsworn: "Two warm marks, and the marked had reflections thrown across the middle to the far corner. Glass dangers fell there; hall dangers fell on the hero. They chose ground clear in both places. I have only ever needed one.",
      thresher: "A warm mark on two of us, dear, and their reflections stand across the very middle of the floor, in the opposite corner, not behind the wall. Glass dangers fall there. So stand where you are safe, and where your opposite corner is safe too.",
      bloodreaver: "A warm mark on two friends and their reflections stood in the opposite corner, straight through the middle of the hall! Glass dangers hit them there, and the hall's hit them where they stood. A double wound, if you picked badly. They picked a spot safe at both ends.",
      runeguard: "A warm mark on two heroes, and their reflections stand across the exact middle of the hall, not across the wall. Glass dangers fall on that far corner; hall dangers on the hero. Choose a spot clear in both, and its opposite clear of the glass.",
      gorgemaw: "Two guests wore a warm mark, and their reflections sat at the opposite end of the table, straight through the middle, not behind the glass. Glass dishes landed there. They chose a seat that was safe, and whose opposite seat was safe too.",
      oathbroker: "Two parties are marked warm, and their reflections reside at the diagonally opposite point, through the centre of the hall, not across the wall. Glass liabilities fall on that point. The marked must choose ground that is safe, and whose opposite is also unencumbered.",
      ironpalm: "A warm mark on two of us, and their reflections stood far across the middle, in the opposite corner, not behind the wall. Glass dangers fell there. They chose a spot clear on both sides, and stood on it.",
      ranger: "Warm mark on two. Their reflection stands diagonally opposite, straight through the middle of the hall, not across the wall. Glass dangers hit that far corner. Pick ground that's clear, with clear ground opposite. Both ends matter.",
      shadowblade: "A warm mark on two of us, and their shadows were thrown clean across the middle, to the opposite corner, not behind the wall. Glass dangers fell there. I picked a spot that was safe, with a safe opposite, and let everyone else do the sums.",
      pyromancer: "A warm mark on two of us, and our reflections were thrown right across the middle, to the opposite corner, not behind the wall! The glass dangers fell there, miles from me. I stood where it was safe, and where the opposite corner was safe too. A neat trick.",
      thermist: "Warm mark on two. Their reflections are across the middle of the hall, in the opposite corner, not behind the wall. Glass dangers fall there. Pick ground that is clear, with clear ground opposite. Both ends. I did the sum cold, and then ran at it hot.",
      driftblade: "A warm mark on two of us, and our reflections were thrown through the middle to the opposite corner, not behind the wall, so glass dangers fell there, far from me, and I ran to a spot where mine was clear and its opposite was too, and never once looked behind.",
      alembist: "Observed: a warm mark on two; their reflections stand diagonally opposite, through the middle of the hall. Result: glass dangers strike that far corner. Method: choose ground that is clear, with clear ground opposite. Label: the mirror is a point, not a wall.",
      threadweaver: "A warm mark on two of my stitches, and their reflections hung across the middle of the hall, in the opposite corner, not behind the wall. Glass dangers fell there. I sent them to a spot that was clear, with a clear opposite, and the seam held.",
      marionettist: "Two players got a warm mark, and their understudies stood in the opposite corner, straight across the middle of the stage, not behind the glass. Glass dangers fell there. They took a mark that was safe, with a safe opposite. (A double blocking.)",
      cleric: "Two of my friends carried a warm mark, and their reflections stood in the opposite corner, straight across the middle, not behind the wall. Glass dangers fell there. I asked each to choose a place that was safe, with a safe place opposite.",
      grovekeeper: "Two of my lot got a warm mark, and the glass threw their reflections to the far corner of the hall, straight across the middle. Anything the glass laid fell on that far corner. They stood where the ground was clear, and where the far ground was clear too.",
      chronomancer: "A warm mark on two, and their reflections stood across the exact middle, in the far corner, not behind the wall. Glass dangers fell there. I will have told them to find ground that was safe at both ends; they did.",
      tidecaller: "Two of us bore a warm mark, and the water showed their reflections in the far corner, across the middle of the hall and not past the wall. Glass dangers broke there. We found ground that was clear, with clear ground opposite.",
      lanternkeeper: "A warm mark over two of us, and their reflections stood across the middle of the hall, in the opposite corner, not behind the wall. Glass dangers fell there. I counted corners: the marked chose a spot that was safe, and whose opposite was lit and clear.",
      kenseier: "Two bear a warm mark, and their reflections stand across the middle of the hall, in the opposite corner, not behind the wall. Glass dangers strike there. The marked choose ground that is clear, with clear ground opposite. A lot to read, one step to take.",
      houndmaster: "Warm mark on two, and their doubles stood across the middle, in the opposite corner, not behind the wall. Glass dangers hit them there. Picked ground that was clear with clear ground opposite. Dogs lay down where they pleased.",
    } },
    'Trading Places': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Midway through the dance the knights changed places. From then on her cone was drawn in the glass and her reflection's long stripe in the hall. I read where each was painted, never whose it was. The glass ones leave a slow wound, and the healer had it to tend.",
      gloomsworn: "The watch changed hands in the middle of the dance. From the fourth cut, her cone was drawn in the glass and her reflection's line across the hall. I stopped asking whose it was and looked at where it was painted. Glass cuts leave a slow wound. The last was still both knights.",
      thresher: "The partners swap sides halfway round the dance, dear: after that her cone is painted in the glass, and the reflection's long line is painted on the floor under our feet. Look at where each is painted, not whose it is, and step out of it. The glass ones leave a slow wound.",
      bloodreaver: "Halfway through, the pair of them changed sides, and from then on the glass held her cone and the hall held the reflection's line! Whose cut it was stopped mattering; where it was painted mattered. The glass ones left aches that lingered, and I took mine gladly. The last still came from both.",
      runeguard: "A rule for the margin: partway through the dance the knights exchange rooms, so a cone of hers is painted in the glass and a line of her reflection's in the hall. The room it is painted in is the room it strikes. Glass cuts leave the slow wound. The last is unchanged.",
      gorgemaw: "Halfway through the tasting the cooks swapped kitchens, and the dish she plated as a cone came out in the glass, her reflection's long slice in the hall. Judge a dish by the plate it is on, not the cook. The glass ones left a lingering ache. The last course came as before.",
      oathbroker: "Mid-dance, jurisdiction is exchanged: her cone is now filed in the glass, her reflection's line in the hall. The instrument binds where it is filed, whoever drew it. A cut filed in the glass leaves a lingering wound. The final cut is joint and unamended.",
      ironpalm: "Halfway through, the two voices changed places. Her cone was spoken in the glass now, her reflection's line in the hall. I listened for where it was spoken, not by whom. The glass ones leave a long, slow wound. The last was still both together.",
      ranger: "Fourth cut on, they swap: cone's painted in the glass, line's painted on the hall floor. Read the paint, not the cutter. Glass cuts leave a slow wound behind. Final cut's still both rooms at once, and the same as before.",
      shadowblade: "Halfway round the dance they swapped places, quite smoothly: her cone went into the glass, her reflection's line into the hall. A mark should be read by where the cut is painted, not by who holds the blade. Glass cuts bleed slowly. The last cut ignored the swap.",
      pyromancer: "Midway through the dance the pair swapped stages! Her cone was now painted in the glass, and the reflection's line right across the hall. I stopped looking at the dancers and watched the paint. Glass cuts leave a slow burn. The last cut came from both, as before, and I took my bow.",
      thermist: "Mid-dance, they swap. Cone into the glass. Line onto the hall floor. Read the paint, not the dancer, and my pulse was going so hard I nearly stepped the wrong way. Glass cuts leave slow wounds. The last cut: both again, same as before.",
      driftblade: "Partway round the dance they swapped rooms, her cone in the glass and her reflection's line across the hall, so I stopped watching their arms and watched the paint, and ran round whichever was painted over me, and the glass ones leave slow wounds, and the last was both as ever.",
      alembist: "Entry: partway through the dance, the labels on the jars swap. Her cone is drawn in the glass; her reflection's line is drawn in the hall. Read the room it is drawn in, not the cutter. Glass cuts leave a slow wound. The final cut is unchanged. Keep a dose for it.",
      threadweaver: "Halfway through, the knights swapped sides of the cloth: her cone was now in the glass and her reflection's line in the hall. I had my stitches read where it was painted, not who held the blade. Glass cuts leave a slow snag, and I mended them. The last cut stayed both.",
      marionettist: "Midway through the number, the stars traded stages: her cone fell in the glass and her understudy's long line across the hall. Watch the lighting, not the performer. A cut in the glass leaves a slow bruise for the healer. The finale was a duet again, unchanged. (The Doll applauded the swap.)",
      cleric: "From the fourth cut on, her cone was drawn in the glass and her reflection's line in the hall, and every cut from the glass left a slow wound for me to tend. I told my friends to watch where it was painted, not whose it was. The last was still both.",
      grovekeeper: "Halfway through the field changed owners: her cone was ploughed into the glass, the reflection's furrow across the hall. Read the ground it is cut in, not who ploughs. The glass ones left a slow wound that I tended through the rest of the dance. The last was still both.",
      chronomancer: "The order flips partway through: her cone lands in the glass, her reflection's line in the hall, the same dance with its tenses swapped. I will have looked at where it was drawn, not whose it was. Glass cuts leave a slow wound. The last cut was both, as it always is.",
      tidecaller: "Partway round the dance the knights traded shores: her cone came down in the glass, her reflection's long line across the hall. I read the water it fell in, not the one who cast it. Glass cuts leave a slow wound, and I eased it as it ebbed. The last was both, as before.",
      lanternkeeper: "Halfway through, the knights swapped rooms. I watched where the red light fell: her cone in the glass, her reflection's line in the hall. Which knight held the blade did not matter. Glass cuts leave a slow wound, and I kept a lamp on whoever took one. The last cut was still both.",
      kenseier: "At the fourth stroke the knights change hands: the cone is painted in the glass, the line in the hall. The count is unchanged; only the room it falls in. Meet each where it is painted. Glass strokes leave a slow wound. The last stroke is still both at once.",
      houndmaster: "Halfway through they swapped sides. Her cone turned up in the glass, her double's line across the hall floor. Rust chased the wrong one and got whistled back. Read the paint, not the knight. Glass cuts leave a slow ache after. Last cut was both again, same as before.",
    } },
  },
};
